WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.300
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.930
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.963
for everyday life.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.370
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.320
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.220
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.773
Today we are talking
about the science of heat,

00:00:17.773 --> 00:00:22.080
and more specifically the
science of heating, the verb,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.470
meaning how our body heats up

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.240
from both the outside and the inside.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.980
Heat is a remarkable stimulus,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.240
meaning when we are in a hot environment,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.530
it has a profound effect on our biology.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.280
And heating up from the outside,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.340
or as you'll soon learn, from the inside,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.720
has a profound effect on
many different aspects

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.750
of our health, including our metabolism,

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.000
both in the immediate and long term;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.900
our cognition, meaning
our ability to think

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.610
more or less clearly.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.370
And if you're immediately
thinking that heating up

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.096
makes you less capable of
thinking, you're wrong.

00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:03.800
Heat applied properly as a stimulus

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.160
can engage certain neurochemical systems

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.340
in your brain and body

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.220
that can allow your brain
to function far better.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.110
We will talk about those data today.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.190
So we're going to talk about

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.040
the science of heat and heating,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.130
both in terms of their mechanisms.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.800
And, as I know many of
you are interested in,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.879
the tools related to the use of heat,

00:01:21.879 --> 00:01:25.510
things like sauna, how often to do sauna,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:26.900
how long to be in the sauna,

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.640
how hot to be in the sauna

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.200
for particular goals and outcomes.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.270
We're also going to talk about
the very exciting new science

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.160
around local heating.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.760
That is the use of heat applied

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.710
to specific areas of the
body in order to heal

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.770
or improve tissues at that
location that you are heating

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.870
as well as your biology
and health overall.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.670
In fact, we are going to talk about one

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.680
very recently published paper

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.630
that came out in the journal Cell.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.730
Cell is one of the three apex journals,

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.960
meaning three of the most competitive,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.130
most rigorous scientific journals.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.100
Those are Nature, Science, and Cell.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.110
This particular paper
was published in Cell,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.380
and I will go into it
in more detail later,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.679
but basically what this paper shows

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:13.679
is that by locally
heating up skin and fat,

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:18.320
you can change the identity
of certain fat cells

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.600
at that location and elsewhere.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.030
We have three kinds of fat,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.810
white fat, beige fat, and brown fat.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.400
And as you will learn more about soon,

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.590
white fat is not very
metabolically active.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.550
It's more of a fuel reserve.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.067
It's what we typically
think of as blubbery fat.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:36.870
Beige fat and brown fat
are rich in mitochondria,

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.350
and those mitochondria
provide a sort of furnace

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.340
or heating mechanism for your entire body

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.960
and increase your metabolism

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.400
and the burning of white fat.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.233
So in other words,

00:02:46.233 --> 00:02:49.030
having more beige fat and
brown fat is a good thing.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.750
And it turns out that
the proper application

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.340
of heat to specific areas of your body

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.900
can increase the conversion
of white fat to beige fat.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.790
In other words, turn an
innocuous fuel source

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.650
into a metabolically active tissue

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.680
that can help you burn off more white fat.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.450
I think many people are going
to be interested in this paper

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.870
and the tools that emerge from this paper.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.670
It's a fascinating set of findings

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.920
that actually emerged
from an understanding

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.290
of the biology of burn and
people who receive intense burns.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.860
And that is not what I'm
going to recommend to you

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.300
as a tool, of course,

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.050
but understanding a
little bit about how burns

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.360
impact our biology and health

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.840
has allowed these pioneering researchers

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.830
to develop new tools to combat

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.610
obesity and metabolic disorders,

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:37.730
and that you can apply for
basic things like fat loss.

