00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:34.260
Okay, let's talk about heat,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.660
more specifically, let's talk about

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.980
the biology of heat and heating

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:39.920
and the health benefits and tools

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.051
related to heat and heating.

00:09:42.051 --> 00:09:44.100
The first question that we have to answer

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.660
is how do we heat up?

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:50.880
And the answer to that question
is we heat up two ways.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.160
We heat up from the outside,

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.540
meaning the things that
we come into contact with,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:57.617
the clothing that we put on our body,

00:09:57.617 --> 00:09:59.970
whether or not there's heat in the room,

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.980
or whether or not it's cold
outside or cold in a room,

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.740
and we heat up from the inside.

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:09.070
Our body has the capacity
to generate more heat

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:12.519
or to cool down, meaning to
turn off the heating process,

00:10:12.519 --> 00:10:15.560
and it can do that in ways

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.154
that match the external environment.

00:10:18.154 --> 00:10:20.080
The simplest way to think about this

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.020
is that we actually have
two body temperatures.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:24.717
People will say, "Oh,
what's body temperature?

00:10:24.717 --> 00:10:25.680
"98.6."

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:27.290
That's actually not true.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.550
Body temperature varies
between individuals.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.260
It varies across time of
day within individuals.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:36.110
And at every point across
your entire lifespan,

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:37.800
you have two distinct temperatures.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.930
One is the temperature on your skin,

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:42.810
what scientists call your shell,

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.340
and the temperature of your core,

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.220
your viscera, meaning your organs,

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.540
your nervous system, and your spinal cord.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:50.770
And as you can imagine,

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.040
the temperature of your core

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:56.090
is always higher than the
temperature at your surface.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:57.150
So the important thing to know

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.580
is that you have a
temperature at your shell

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.050
and a temperature at your core.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:02.380
Now you don't need to know exactly

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.330
what those temperatures are in most cases,

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:07.220
but it is vitally important to understand

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.980
that you have those two temperatures

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.950
and that your brain is constantly

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:12.833
sending out signals to your body

00:11:12.833 --> 00:11:16.810
as to whether or not it
should heat up or cool down,

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:19.780
depending on the temperature of the shell,

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:21.020
which makes total sense.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:23.770
This is a lot like a thermostat in a room,

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.640
which is essentially paying attention

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.060
to how cold or hot it is,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.640
and then sending signals to
the heating or cooling system

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.020
to either heat up the environment

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.420
or cool down the environment,

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:35.252
depending on the temperature
in that environment.

00:11:35.252 --> 00:11:38.850
Your brain has neurons that send signals

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:40.530
to other cells in your body

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.150
and deploy the release of
chemicals in your brain and body

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.340
to heat you up when you are too cold

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:48.140
and to cool you down when you are too hot.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.080
So, if you can understand

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.740
that you have two body temperatures,

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:53.120
one at your shell, the surface,

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.090
and one at your core, inside,

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:57.460
and that your body and
brain are always trying

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:00.680
to balance those two temperatures
in the appropriate way,

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:01.970
well then you're halfway there

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.430
to understanding the biology

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:05.220
of thermal regulation and heating,

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.411
and you'll be a lot further along

00:12:07.411 --> 00:12:10.840
in understanding how
specific tools can be used

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:14.870
to improve metabolism or
improve cognition, for instance.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:17.100
In fact, later you will learn

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.570
that one way that you can heat up

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:21.850
is by cooling down the
surface of your body.

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:22.750
That's right.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.280
If I were to throw a cold
towel, ice cold towel,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.047
onto your torso right now and ask you,

00:12:28.047 --> 00:12:28.880
"Well, how do you feel?"

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:30.810
You'd say, "Oh, that's
cold, that's chilly."

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.230
However, because your brain is acting like

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:35.240
a bit of a thermostat as the surface,

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.777
the shell of your body felt cool,

00:12:37.777 --> 00:12:39.970
it would make sense that that thermostat

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.810
would activate biological mechanisms

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:43.733
that would heat up your core.

00:12:43.733 --> 00:12:47.500
Similarly, if I were to put you
into a very hot environment,

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.100
you'd say, "Oh, wow, it's
really, really warm in here,"

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.860
but your brain and your body would go

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.280
through a lot of effort
to activate mechanisms

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:55.810
to cool you down.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:57.280
So anytime we're talking about heat,

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.200
meaning deliberate heat
exposure, things like sauna,

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:03.850
it's very important to
understand, not just the stimulus,

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.860
how hot something is, how long
you're in a sauna, et cetera,

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:10.600
but the effect that has on
your shell and on your core.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:13.200
If you can understand that
you can design protocols

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.950
that are literally perfect for your goals.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:17.650
And as a final point about this,

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.380
if you want to develop the best tools,

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:23.530
leveraging heat for your biology
and health and performance,

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.250
you want to understand heat as a process,

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:30.080
as a verb, as heating, not just heat,

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:33.040
because there's the
temperature that you are at

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.630
before you encounter the heat stimulus,

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:37.630
before you get in the sauna, for instance,

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:40.690
during the heat stimulus, so
while you're in the sauna,

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.460
and then afterward.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.470
Everything in biology is a process.

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:45.460
So as you'll soon learn,

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:49.140
there is a specific sauna
protocol that can allow you,

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:50.460
can allow anybody, in fact,

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:52.470
to increase the amount of growth hormone

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:55.010
released into their
brain and body 16 fold.

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:57.350
That's right, 16 fold.

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:02.250
However, it involves shifting
from a hot environment

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:04.300
to a cool environment,
to a hot environment,

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:06.680
to a cool environment, over
and over and over again,

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.650
over a very short period of time,

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.350
because it engages a switch, a process,

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.370
that compounds, it builds on itself,

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.800
to increase growth hormone
further and further.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:17.900
In fact, if you were to
just get into a sauna

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:21.050
for a very long period of time
and crank up the temperature

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:24.450
to match the exact temperature
that was used in that study,

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:27.870
you would not experience those
increases in growth hormone.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.200
It really is the transition

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:31.270
between hot and cool temperatures

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:33.720
that engage the process of heating

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.770
and reheating over and over again.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.630
So, today you're going to
learn about the use of sauna.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:40.970
You're going to learn about the
use of other heat-related tools

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:42.600
for health and optimization,

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:43.710
not just for growth hormone,

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:45.900
but also metabolic health,
for controlling cortisol,

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:49.800
even to impact mental
health in positive ways.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:51.380
And in order to do that,

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:54.010
you need to understand a
little bit about the mechanisms

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:57.060
of how you heat up and how you cool down,

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:59.580
where the cells and circuits
are in the brain and body,

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.320
how those cells and circuits work.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:02.940
I promise to make the description of that

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:04.510
which follows very clear,

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:06.734
even if you don't have
a background in biology.

00:15:06.734 --> 00:15:08.760
And once you have that in hand,

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.260
along with the understanding you now have

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.210
about the fact that you
got a shell and a core,

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:15.700
and you need to think about
both the shell and the core,

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:18.310
well, then you will be in
the best possible position

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:21.190
to use sauna or hot tub or other tools,

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:22.570
even just a hot shower,

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:25.950
as a powerful stimulus
to optimize your biology.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.470
Now, the science of heat and heating

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:30.170
and cold and cooling, for that matter,

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.420
goes back well over a hundred years.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:33.910
In fact, it's kind of amusing to me

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:36.310
that nowadays there's a
kind of renewed interest

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:37.710
in the use of heat and cold

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:39.440
and the science of heat and cold,

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.830
because this was the
first topic that I studied

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:43.060
as an undergraduate.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:46.780
And in fact, I did my graduate
thesis on thermal regulation.

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:49.960
And at the time, thermal
regulation wasn't really considered

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:52.220
one of the hot topics in neuroscience.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.540
People were more focused on things

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.170
like memory and consciousness.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.930
And of course those topics
are still of vital interest

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.550
to many people in many laboratories,

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:01.480
but thermal regulation was considered

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.190
more a thing for the physiologists.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:06.940
Nowadays, not just on social media,

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:09.920
not just in the landscape
of biohackers and athletes,

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:12.450
but in the landscape of
mental health, and frankly,

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:17.020
in the general ethos
around health optimization,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:19.930
people are really
interested in heat and cold.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.140
And the reason they're so
interested in heat and cold

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.020
is that a lot of the science has been done

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.680
both in animal models
in mice and in humans

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:30.810
and translates immediately to
protocols that anyone can use.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:35.330
Now, a brief warning now and
another brief warning later,

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:38.100
anytime you're talking
about heating up your body,

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.320
you need to be very cautious,

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:41.580
because unlike cooling down,

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:44.420
where you have a fairly broad
range of cold temperatures

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:47.250
that you can go into before
it's damaging to tissue,

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.500
well, you don't get to heat up
the brain and body very much

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:53.810
before you start getting into
the realm of neuron damage,

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:55.880
and neuron's in the
central nervous system,

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:56.750
the brain and spinal cord,

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:58.756
once they're damaged they don't come back.

00:16:58.756 --> 00:17:01.360
So hyperthermia is a
serious thing to avoid.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:02.730
Later I'll talk about ways

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:06.020
to rapidly protect against hyperthermia,

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:09.230
but I do want to give everybody
a cautionary note up front.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:11.160
Obviously, if you're pregnant, nursing,

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.410
if you're very sensitive
to hot environments,

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.970
you want to stay out of saunas
and things of that sort.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:17.380
I'm sure there are exceptions to that.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:19.492
You definitely have to talk to your doctor

00:17:19.492 --> 00:17:22.720
if you're going to violate that rule.

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.180
And for everybody, you want to approach

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:28.860
any kind of tool related
to heating very cautiously.

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.470
You always have the opportunity

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:31.680
to increase the temperature later.

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:32.860
So proceed with caution.

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:33.693
Be smart about it.

00:17:33.693 --> 00:17:34.930
I don't just say of that to protect me.

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:37.090
I say that also to protect you.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:38.840
So now let's talk about

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:40.900
what are the circuits for heating up?

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:42.280
How does that happen?

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:44.190
Many of you have probably
experienced a fever.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.370
How does that happen?

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:48.170
What happens when you go
into a cold environment

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.010
and you're shivering,

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.850
but you put on a coat
and then you feel warmer.

00:17:51.850 --> 00:17:53.410
What's really going on there?

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.150
Well, there's a very basic circuit,

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:59.790
meaning neurons that exist in the skin,

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.130
in the brain, and in the body

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:04.070
that communicate with one
another that allow you

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.950
to heat up if you need to
and cool down if you need to.

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:08.840
I'm going to throw a
little bit of nomenclature,

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:10.240
a few new words at you.

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:13.470
You don't need to memorize
these words, except for one,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.240
actually you need to memorize one acronym,

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:16.960
but it's very easy.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:18.070
It's called the POA.

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:19.732
If you remember POA,

00:18:19.732 --> 00:18:21.410
you'll be home free for
the rest of the episode,

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.360
but I know that there are
some aficionados out there

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.030
and people interested in getting

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:26.410
a little bit deeper mechanism.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:28.330
And I do think it's important
to understand the circuit,

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:29.940
because once you stand this circuit

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:31.690
and the way it's structured,

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.300
then you are going to be in a
great position to use the tools

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:35.290
related to heating.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.570
So here's how this circuit is structured.

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:40.350
You have this shell,
which is basically skin,

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:43.420
and within the skin you
have neurons, nerve cells.

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:47.450
Those nerve cells have
channels or receptors on them.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:48.660
They're called trip channels.

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.010
There's some other ones as well,

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:53.290
which basically sense changes in heat.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:57.140
So if I were to put a hot
object on your hand or your arm,

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:58.610
or for instance,

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:00.780
if I were to put a hot
object on your hand or arm,

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:03.530
and then remove that hot object,

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:05.240
those neurons would respond to that.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:07.810
They would send electrical
signals into your spinal cord.

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:10.790
And that's where the next
station of the circuit resides.

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:13.780
In your spinal cord you got
a little cluster of neurons

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:16.460
that exists at the top
part of your spinal cord

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:18.130
called the dorsal horn.

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:19.340
The name, again, doesn't matter.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:22.610
And those neurons specifically
relay heat information

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:24.110
up to another area of your brain.

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:26.020
Now here's where we get
into some fancy names.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:29.370
It's the lateral parabrachial area.

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:31.410
You don't need to know
lateral parabrachial area,

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:32.820
but it's a relay station.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.610
The lateral parabrachial area

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:37.510
sends electrical signals to the POA.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:39.000
And I would like you to know POA.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.840
The POA stands for preoptic area.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.770
Neurons in the preoptic area

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:45.970
basically reside over
the roof of your mouth.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.165
These are neurons within the hypothalamus,

00:19:48.165 --> 00:19:51.184
and neurons in the preoptic area

00:19:51.184 --> 00:19:54.570
have the ability to send signals

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:56.400
out to the rest of your brain and body

00:19:56.400 --> 00:20:00.540
to get you to heat up and
actually to change your behavior

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:02.220
so that you heat up.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:03.570
That's right.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:05.650
If neurons in the preoptic area

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.540
receive an electrical signal

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:08.930
through the circuit I just described,

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:11.820
that goes from skin to dorsal
horn of the spinal cord,

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:13.663
to lateral parabrachial,

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:16.470
they will start sending signals

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:19.440
out to the organs of your body
and the tissues of your body

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:21.620
to get those organs and
tissues to do things.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.570
And believe it or not, your
POA, your preoptic area,

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:25.940
will actually change the way

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:28.960
that you think and feel immediately.

00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:33.900
For instance, if something
warm contacts your skin,

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.040
or something very hot contacts your skin,

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:38.322
the preoptic area will send signals

00:20:38.322 --> 00:20:42.350
out to the endothelial cells,

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:45.720
the blood vessels both
of the brain and body

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:47.870
that get them to dilate,

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:51.320
to essentially increase their
volume and their surface area

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.283
in order to cast off heat.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:56.390
You will also start sweating.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:58.670
That sweating response is initiated,

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.430
not by the hot day or the hot sun,

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:03.510
but by the preoptic area neurons

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.660
that send signals out to what's called

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:07.180
the periphery of your

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.640
and other chemicals are released,

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:11.573
things like acetylcholine,
that get you to sweat.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:15.770
And if you happen to be shivering,

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:17.170
neurons in the preoptic area

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.943
will make sure that you stop shivering.

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:23.110
You're probably familiar with the feeling

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:24.590
of being somewhat lethargic,

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.140
or spreading out your limbs on a hot day.

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:31.390
Well, that is the result of
neurons in your preoptic area

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.510
impacting your musculature

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:36.030
to get you to increase your surface area

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:39.310
so you can sweat off or release more heat.

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:40.900
So there are all these
different mechanisms

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:43.060
by which we dump heat.

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:46.150
Some of those are purely physiological,

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:48.050
below our conscious control,

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:49.970
things like sweating,

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:53.280
which you can't just make
yourself sweat on demand.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:55.330
Maybe you can through a
set of stressful thoughts,

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:57.385
but you can't just make yourself sweat.

00:21:57.385 --> 00:22:01.030
That is autonomic, it's
below your conscious control.

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:02.644
Things like vasodilation,

00:22:02.644 --> 00:22:06.650
the dilation of your veins in particular

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:08.450
and capillaries in particular.

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:09.360
These sorts of things.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:11.760
And, of course, there
are these behavioral,

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:15.010
somewhat voluntary
aspects of dumping heat.

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:17.870
And the lethargy, the kind
of tiredness that we feel

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:19.400
on a really hot day,

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:21.040
that's also controlled by the circuit

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:22.280
that I just described.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:26.750
In fact, I just got back from
a visit to a very warm place,

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:29.980
and it was remarkable to
me how lethargic I felt

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:30.813
in the afternoons.

00:22:30.813 --> 00:22:32.730
I just felt like a total slug.

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:36.321
I just could not move
or rally to do anything,

00:22:36.321 --> 00:22:39.540
except if I waited until the evening,

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.270
even though it was later in the day,

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:43.010
even though I hadn't napped,

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:45.110
as the temperature in my
environment cooled off,

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:46.740
as my body temperature cooled off,

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:47.830
I felt like I had more energy.

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:48.910
I was actually waking up,

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:50.940
even though I had been awake for longer.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:53.430
So the relationship between
temperature and lethargy

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:55.600
is a very intimate one.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.070
If we're warm enough, we feel active

00:22:58.070 --> 00:22:59.720
and like we want to move around.

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:00.980
If we're too warm,

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:02.690
we feel like we need to stay put

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.210
and spread out our limbs and dump heat.

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.640
And that brings me to a
quick and kind of fun point

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:10.690
about how we dump heat versus
how other animals dump heat.

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:14.510
Many of you know, of course,
that we dump heat by sweating.

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:16.970
Other mechanisms as well,
some of which I described,

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:19.430
but that's our main way of dumping heat.

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:22.390
Other animals like dogs don't
have the capacity to sweat,

00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:23.570
at least not very much,

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:26.780
so they pant in order to dump heat.

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:29.040
And still other animals, like rodents,

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:32.150
when they get too hot
they spit on their paws

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:35.050
and they rub that spit on
the surface of their body,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:36.170
which might sound kind of gross,

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:37.470
and probably will get you to think twice

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:38.900
before petting any of those animals

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:40.790
or holding any of those animals again,

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:42.670
unless that's your thing.

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:44.280
Now, one other key thing to understand

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:46.450
about this circuit related to heat

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:47.900
is that the preoptic area

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:51.120
also can send electrical
signals to the amygdala,

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.950
a brain area that is often talked about

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:55.510
in the context of fear,

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:57.430
but is really just a brain area

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:00.080
that can activate your
sympathetic nervous system.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:01.860
The sympathetic nervous system

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.110
is part of your autonomic nervous system

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:06.340
and is the one associated
with fight or flight,

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:08.150
or with the stress response,

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.340
or even just the excited response.

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:12.410
The sympathetic nervous system
is also what gets activated

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:14.160
when you're really
excited about something.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:17.010
The preoptic area has the opportunity

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:19.760
to trigger the activation of the amygdala.

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:22.150
Now, it doesn't do it
every time, but it can.

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:24.190
And it tends to do that
when you are suddenly

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:26.940
in an environment that feels too hot,

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:30.070
that you feel is risky levels of hot.

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:32.180
If you ever have gotten into a sauna

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:35.650
that was very, very hot,
maybe 210 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:37.510
you sit there for a minute.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:39.560
You'll notice that your
heart rate increases,

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:40.400
and there are reasons for that,

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.270
and we'll talk about some of
the health benefits of that

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:45.788
in a few minutes, but
it's pretty uncomfortable.

00:24:45.788 --> 00:24:48.640
You may not feel like your
skin is going to burn up,

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:51.630
but you often will feel
the impulse to get out,

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:53.890
especially if you stay in
there for a little while.

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:57.890
That impulse is the consequence
of this preoptic area

00:24:57.890 --> 00:24:59.407
communicating with your amygdala saying,

00:24:59.407 --> 00:25:01.150
"Hey, this environment is really hot,

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:02.120
and I'm tryin' to cool down,

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:03.500
and it's not really working.

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:06.520
I'm dumping heat, but
I'm not able to adjust

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:07.910
the core of my body temperature

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:09.800
in ways that are going
to protect my neurons.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:11.870
And so it's a signal that you probably

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:13.860
shouldn't stay in that
environment too long.

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:15.330
Now later we'll talk about the advantage

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:17.190
of pushing yourself a little bit through

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:18.570
some of these very hot environments,

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:20.200
provided you can do it safely,

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:22.410
but the impulse to get yourself

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.630
out of a very hot environment

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:26.970
is the consequence of the POA

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:28.710
communicating with your amygdala,

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:30.100
and the amygdala then, in turn,

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.680
activating your adrenal glands,

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:33.610
which sit right above your kidneys,

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:34.850
the release of adrenaline,

00:25:34.850 --> 00:25:37.600
and this feeling of agitation
like you want to move.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.340
Usually you want to move out
of whatever hot environment

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:41.470
you happen to be in.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:42.870
So now you know the circuit.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:43.703
Again, it's simple.

00:25:43.703 --> 00:25:45.630
It goes from skin to spinal cord,

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:47.640
one brain area to another brain area.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:49.350
That's the key one in this discussion,

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.592
which is the POA, the preoptic area.

00:25:51.592 --> 00:25:53.290
And then the preop area can kick off

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:57.610
a bunch of autonomic
subconscious responses to heat,

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:00.060
which make us attempt to get cooler,

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:02.380
things like sweating,
vasodilation, et cetera,

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:05.170
and it can kick off behavioral responses,

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:06.800
spreading out our limbs in an attempt

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:08.430
to dump even more heat;

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:12.010
feeling lethargic, so a lack
of desire to run and move;

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:14.830
and it also has the
opportunity to kick off

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:16.770
a mild, or maybe not so mild,

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:19.540
panic response to get us
out of that hot environment.

00:26:19.540 --> 00:26:21.330
If you can conceptualize that circuit,

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:23.710
or if you can even just
understand what I just said,

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:25.740
even at a top contour level,

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:27.090
you're going to be in a great position

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:28.887
to understand the rest of the information

00:26:28.887 --> 00:26:30.490
and the tools that follow.

00:26:30.490 --> 00:26:32.370
Next, I'd like to talk about the use

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:35.030
of deliberate heat
exposure, including sauna,

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:37.092
but other tools as well,

00:26:37.092 --> 00:26:39.880
as a way to understand how heat

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:42.750
and heating changes our biology.

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:44.400
So, you're going to learn some mechanism

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:46.120
and you're going to learn some tools.

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:48.190
But first I'd like to just emphasize

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:50.700
that the use of deliberate heat exposure

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:55.130
can be a very powerful way to
improve health and longevity.

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:56.493
There's a wonderful study on this

00:26:56.493 --> 00:26:59.910
that was published in 2018 that includes

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:02.330
a lot of data from a lot of participants,

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:04.040
in a lot of different conditions,

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:06.010
for instance, people
that only did sauna once

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:07.450
versus two to three times a week

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:09.600
versus four to seven
times a week and so on,

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.180
and compares all those.

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:14.410
The title of the study is
Sauna Bathing is Associated

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:16.570
With Reduced Cardiovascular Mortality

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:19.640
and Improves Risk
Prediction in Men and Women

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:22.100
a Prospective Cohort Study.

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:26.160
This is one of several papers
that clearly demonstrate

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:29.030
that regular use of sauna or other forms

00:27:29.030 --> 00:27:31.110
of deliberate heat exposure

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:34.030
can reduce mortality to
cardiovascular events,

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:35.920
but also to other events,

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:38.300
things like stroke and other things

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.477
that basically can kill us.

00:27:40.477 --> 00:27:43.260
What I like so much about
this and the related studies,

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:46.380
and yes, I will provide a link
to these in the show notes,

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:48.640
is that they involve
a lot of participants.

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:50.230
So for instance, in this particular paper,

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.163
which was published in BMC Medicine,

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:58.095
they looked at a sample
of 1,688 participants

00:27:58.095 --> 00:28:00.300
who had a mean age of 63,

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:03.410
but there was a range of ages around 63,

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:06.360
and of whom 51.4% were women.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:07.250
The rest were men.

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:09.260
So it's a pretty nicely varied study

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:12.210
in terms of the populations
that they looked at.

00:28:12.210 --> 00:28:13.810
And basically what they found

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:17.121
was the more often that people do sauna,

00:28:17.121 --> 00:28:19.010
the better their health is

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:22.100
and the lower the likelihood they will die

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:24.223
from some sort of cardiovascular event.

00:28:24.223 --> 00:28:25.810
What do we mean by sauna?

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:28.690
We need to define some of
the parameters around sauna,

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:32.180
and I promise to provide
you some alternative ways

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:34.150
to access some of the health benefits

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:36.610
that were observed in
this and related studies

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:38.280
without the need to have a sauna,

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:40.010
'cause I do realize that a lot of people

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:42.210
don't have access to sauna.

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:44.490
First off, the temperature
ranges that were used

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:46.790
in this and pretty much all the studies

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:47.900
that I'm going to talk about,

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:51.980
unless I say otherwise, are
between 80 degrees Celsius,

00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:54.610
meaning 176 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:57.310
and 100 degrees Celsius,

00:28:57.310 --> 00:29:00.300
meaning 212 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:02.300
So somewhere in that range.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:05.030
How hot should you make the
sauna or the environment

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:07.910
that you get into should you
decide to use these tools?

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:11.470
Well, that will depend on
your tolerance for heat,

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:13.130
how heat adapted you are.

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:15.540
Yes, some people are better
at sweating than others.

00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:17.590
And over time we all
get better at sweating.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:19.490
Meaning if you go into
the sauna more frequently,

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:22.630
you become a better sweater,
not sweater you wear,

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:24.290
but the verb sweater,

00:29:24.290 --> 00:29:25.530
you get better at sweating,

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.918
at dumping heat through the loss of water.

00:29:27.918 --> 00:29:29.940
So it's going to depend.

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:32.030
I recommend starting on the lower end

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:33.310
of the temperature scale,

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:34.680
and if that's too hot for you

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:36.660
that you even lower the
temperature further.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:39.400
Now, how long were people
exposing themselves

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:40.700
to these hot environments?

00:29:41.620 --> 00:29:45.360
Anywhere from five to
20 minutes per session.

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:46.740
And as you'll soon learn,

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:49.790
very brief periods of just
five minutes of heat exposure

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:51.610
can be a powerful stimulus

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:56.610
if the heat exposure is
significantly great enough for you.

00:29:58.594 --> 00:30:00.270
20 minutes can also be beneficial,

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:03.530
but 80 to 100 degrees Celsius,

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:07.310
meaning 176 degrees Fahrenheit
to 212 degrees Fahrenheit

00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:10.811
is the general range that
this and most studies use.

00:30:10.811 --> 00:30:12.570
In this particular study,

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:14.440
they compared the effects of people

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:16.331
that did sauna once a week,

00:30:16.331 --> 00:30:18.240
two or three times per week,

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:20.020
or four to seven times per week.

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:22.410
And what they saw was really remarkable.

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:23.740
What they observed was that people

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:27.050
who went into the sauna
two or three times per week

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:31.600
were 27% less likely to die
of a cardiovascular event

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:33.940
than people that went into
the sauna just once a week,

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:36.230
again at the temperature levels

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:38.580
and the duration that
I talked about earlier.

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:39.897
And as you can imagine, the duration,

00:30:39.897 --> 00:30:42.416
the temperature levels were related.

00:30:42.416 --> 00:30:45.010
So if people went into
very hot environments

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:46.220
that were really uncomfortable for them,

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:47.890
maybe they only went in for five minutes.

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:50.980
Whereas, if they were more
comfortable and heat adapted

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:53.300
in a given environment or
their tolerance for heat

00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:55.620
was just simply higher
for whatever reason,

00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:57.760
well then they tended to stay in longer.

00:30:57.760 --> 00:30:58.960
We can take a sort of average

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:00.680
of this five to 20 minute range,

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:03.410
and today we're mainly going
to talk about exposures

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:07.500
between 10 and 20 minutes
at temperatures between,

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:09.570
again, 80 degrees and 100 degrees Celsius,

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:13.170
176 degrees Fahrenheit,
or 212 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:15.470
So these data point to the
fact that going in the sauna

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:17.270
two or three times per
week is really beneficial

00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:20.640
and can lower mortality
to cardiovascular events.

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:22.260
And in fact, the benefits
were even greater

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:23.710
for people that we're going into the sauna

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:25.710
four to seven times per week.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:28.740
Those people were 50% less likely to die

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:29.950
of a cardiovascular event

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:31.860
compared to people that
went into the sauna

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:33.430
just once a week.

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:35.930
So these are really
impressive, and frankly,

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:37.270
encouraging studies.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:39.300
Certainly they caught
my eye and encouraged me

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:43.213
to start using deliberate heat
exposure on a regular basis.

00:31:43.213 --> 00:31:45.540
What's particularly nice about this study,

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:47.160
and the related study that, again,

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:49.690
is linked in the show notes,

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:51.510
is that they looked at a number

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.680
of potentially confounding variables,

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:55.510
things like whether or not people smoked,

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:57.210
things like whether or not
people were overweight,

00:31:57.210 --> 00:32:00.430
whether or not they tended
to exercise or not exercise,

00:32:00.430 --> 00:32:03.110
and they were able to
separate out those variables.

00:32:03.110 --> 00:32:05.130
So the percentages that
I described earlier,

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:09.290
27% less likely to die
of a cardiovascular event

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:11.377
for those that went in the
sauna two to three times a week

00:32:11.377 --> 00:32:13.870
and 50% less likely to die
of a cardiovascular event

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:16.100
for those that went into the
sauna four times per week,

00:32:16.100 --> 00:32:17.970
as compared to just once a week.

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:19.930
Those effects really do seem to be

00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:22.620
the consequence of the sauna exposure

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.060
and not some other effect

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:25.800
that's correlated with sauna exposure,

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:28.110
like going to the gym where people

00:32:28.110 --> 00:32:29.560
are working out seven times a week

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:31.290
and then also happen
to get into the sauna,

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:33.220
or quitting smoking
right about the same time

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:36.350
they adopt a sauna protocol,
these sorts of things.

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:38.530
And now there have been
additional analyses

00:32:38.530 --> 00:32:40.730
of the use of sauna for improving health,

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:43.672
or I should say for offsetting mortality,

00:32:43.672 --> 00:32:46.720
that have found that
it's not just reductions

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:47.860
in cardiovascular events,

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:50.250
but so called all-cause mortality.

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:53.240
This is kind of medical
geek speak for saying,

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:56.150
how likely are you or somebody to die

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:58.030
from a cardiovascular event,

00:32:58.030 --> 00:33:00.930
but maybe be also from some other event,

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:02.300
some other health-related event,

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:04.370
like cancer or something of that sort.

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:05.830
And in every case,

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:07.850
regular exposure to sauna starting

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:09.810
at about two or three times per week,

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.080
all the way up to seven times per week,

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:13.540
greatly improves,

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:18.060
meaning statistically significant
improvements in longevity

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:20.510
in the sense that people are less likely

00:33:20.510 --> 00:33:23.230
to die of cardiovascular events

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:25.360
and other things that kill us.

00:33:25.360 --> 00:33:26.590
So I and many other people

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:28.420
who are interested not
just in our own health,

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:31.370
but in educating about
health-related tools

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:34.107
to the general public,
find this really exciting.

00:33:34.107 --> 00:33:39.107
But knowing what we know about
how heat impacts our biology,

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:41.549
it probably shouldn't surprise us

00:33:41.549 --> 00:33:43.930
that this sauna type exposure

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:46.660
or deliberate heat exposure
has these incredible effects.

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:49.050
So before we get into
the biological mechanisms

00:33:49.050 --> 00:33:52.700
of how heat can have all these
impressive health effects,

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:56.547
I want to just talk about
the use of sauna as a tool

00:33:56.547 --> 00:34:00.260
and emphasize that you
don't have to use a sauna

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:02.100
in order to get these benefits.

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:05.720
It is simply a matter of
making sure that your shell

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:10.550
and your core heat up properly a bit,

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:12.880
not too much, not too little,

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:13.713
but that you heat those up.

00:34:13.713 --> 00:34:16.590
And no, you do not need to
carry a thermometer around

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:18.923
or place a thermometer into your core.

00:34:18.923 --> 00:34:22.080
In laboratory studies and in humans,

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:24.120
if you really want to know
someone's core temperature,

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:25.750
basically you try and
put the thermal probe

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:26.920
as close to the core as you can.

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:30.020
So typically that's done
rectally or a mouth thermometer

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:31.490
or even up the nose.

00:34:31.490 --> 00:34:32.842
You don't need to do any of that.

00:34:32.842 --> 00:34:34.320
This isn't a laboratory study.

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:37.690
There are ways to create a hot environment

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:41.060
such that you heat up your
shell and your core safely

00:34:41.060 --> 00:34:44.193
without having to measure your
core temperature all along.

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:46.840
If you want to do that, be my guess,

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:49.210
but I'm not going to provide a protocol.

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:51.640
So the question is, how are you
heating up your environment?

00:34:51.640 --> 00:34:54.390
And I realize that there are dry saunas,

00:34:54.390 --> 00:34:57.720
there are steam saunas,
there are infrared saunas,

00:34:57.720 --> 00:34:59.010
there are hot tubs,

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:02.613
and there are simply rooms
that you crank up the heat.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:05.620
There are also ways in which you can

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:08.010
increase your shell and
your core temperature

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.390
by moving around a lot

00:35:09.390 --> 00:35:11.513
and doing that wearing a lot of clothing.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.210
There's nothing special about

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:17.100
any one of these approaches or protocols.

00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:19.031
It just so happens that sauna

00:35:19.031 --> 00:35:21.660
is one of the more
convenient ways to do this.

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:24.350
And certainly for the studies
that I've talked about,

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:25.780
not just the ones I referenced before,

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:27.590
but all the studies that I researched

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:29.130
looking at this episode,

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:30.780
it makes sense why they would use sauna,

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:32.860
because it's very hard, for instance,

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:35.108
to create conditions where you have

00:35:35.108 --> 00:35:37.700
five people go out jogging,

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:39.320
wearing heavy sweaters and hats,

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:41.070
wool hats on the middle of summer,

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:42.830
it's very hard to set up those conditions

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:44.480
in a way that's controlled for everybody.

00:35:44.480 --> 00:35:45.920
Whereas, it's pretty straightforward

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:47.190
to have a sauna where you have

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:48.710
one or several people just get into

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:51.240
that one uniformly hot environment.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:53.920
That's a much easier study to run.

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:58.280
So just to be clear, the
temperature range is important.

00:35:58.280 --> 00:36:00.670
You want to get between 80
and 100 degrees Celsius.

00:36:00.670 --> 00:36:02.903
Now you know the conversion to Fahrenheit.

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:06.200
You could, however, immerse yourself

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:08.910
in a hot tub or hot water
bath up to your neck.

00:36:08.910 --> 00:36:10.660
That's another way to approach it.

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:12.960
If you didn't have access
to either of those,

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:17.960
you could also put on a hoodie
or a wool hat and a hoodie,

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:21.450
or you could do like the wrestlers do,

00:36:21.450 --> 00:36:24.170
and you could actually buy
one of these plastic suits.

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:25.270
They're literally called plastics

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:27.710
that wrestlers or other athletes

00:36:27.710 --> 00:36:29.460
that wish to drop water weight will wear,

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:30.580
and then go jogging in that.

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:31.947
All of those will increase your shell

00:36:31.947 --> 00:36:33.540
and your core body temperature.

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:34.650
Especially if you do it on a hot day,

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:36.620
but of course be careful,
hydrate and don't overheat.

00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:38.530
Don't become excessively hyperthermic,

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:41.899
'cause you can get heat stroke
and you can potentially die.

00:36:41.899 --> 00:36:44.860
But if you're going to use sauna,

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:47.260
often I get the question
how hot should the sauna be?

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:48.093
Well now you know.

00:36:48.093 --> 00:36:48.970
How long should you be in there?

00:36:48.970 --> 00:36:51.280
Five to 20 minutes per session.

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.320
Although, I will talk
in a minute about ways

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:54.890
to optimize hormone output,

00:36:54.890 --> 00:36:56.230
in particular growth hormone output,

00:36:56.230 --> 00:36:59.403
by doing four very brief sessions.

00:36:59.403 --> 00:37:01.070
So maybe not a continuous session.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:02.330
We'll get into that in a few minutes.

00:37:02.330 --> 00:37:03.950
And, of course, you have to ask yourself

00:37:03.950 --> 00:37:05.060
wet sauna, dry sauna.

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:05.893
You know what?

00:37:05.893 --> 00:37:06.726
Doesn't matter.

00:37:06.726 --> 00:37:08.170
Use what you prefer.

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.782
Many people ask me, "Well,
what about infrared sauna?"

00:37:10.782 --> 00:37:13.480
We have an entire episode
all about the use of light

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:17.680
and low level light therapy,
including infrared light.

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:19.677
It does have certain benefits for skin

00:37:19.677 --> 00:37:23.526
and other organs and tissues
of the body, if used properly.

00:37:23.526 --> 00:37:26.330
My understanding, or
at least my assessment,

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:28.540
of most infrared saunas out there

00:37:28.540 --> 00:37:30.700
is that they don't get hot enough.

00:37:30.700 --> 00:37:34.530
They don't get up to that 80
to 100 degrees Celsius range.

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:36.730
Some do, most don't.

00:37:36.730 --> 00:37:38.610
So what you end up with is a situation

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:39.910
where you've got a red light,

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:42.160
low level light therapy stimulus,

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:44.547
and you've got a sauna
that's not quite hot enough.

00:37:44.547 --> 00:37:46.600
And there are a lot of ideas and claims

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.480
about how they work together

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:50.483
in order to get you improved benefits.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:53.200
I personally am of the stance,

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:54.760
based on the literature that I've read,

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:57.470
that you want to get into those ranges

00:37:57.470 --> 00:37:59.728
of 80 to 100 degrees Celsius

00:37:59.728 --> 00:38:02.120
before you start
considering whether or not

00:38:02.120 --> 00:38:04.419
you're also going to include
red light therapies, et cetera.

00:38:04.419 --> 00:38:07.660
So there's nothing special
about red light sauna.

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:10.150
It's really the temperature of the sauna

00:38:10.150 --> 00:38:11.250
that you happen to get into.

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:12.840
So which tool?

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:13.673
Which sauna?

00:38:13.673 --> 00:38:14.560
Which stimulus?

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.670
Do you run wearing plastics
and a hoodie and a wool hat

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:18.503
or do you get into a sauna?

00:38:18.503 --> 00:38:20.200
That's going to depend a
lot on your circumstances,

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:22.890
your budget, and what you have
access to on a regular basis.

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:26.350
This is a lot like our
discussion about the use of cold.

00:38:26.350 --> 00:38:29.920
Most of the studies have looked
at immersion in cold water

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:30.753
up to the neck,

00:38:30.753 --> 00:38:32.180
because that's a very controlled situation

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:33.660
that you can do in a laboratory.

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:36.770
They have not explored
cold showers as much,

00:38:36.770 --> 00:38:37.810
So there's just less data,

00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:39.660
or walking around in a cold environment.

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:41.979
But we'll talk a little
bit about those data,

00:38:41.979 --> 00:38:44.340
because as you'll soon learn,
when you talk about cold,

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:46.630
you're actually talking
about heating as well.

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:48.830
So what kind of mechanisms are activated

00:38:48.830 --> 00:38:49.700
in your brain and body

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:52.819
that allow for the various health benefits

00:38:52.819 --> 00:38:56.921
of sauna or other forms
of deliberate heating?

00:38:56.921 --> 00:39:00.300
Well, we talked about reduced risk

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:02.400
of cardiovascular event related mortality

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:04.020
and all-cause mortality.

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:05.110
As you'll soon learn,

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:07.860
there are also tremendous benefits

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:10.450
in terms of increases in growth hormone,

00:39:10.450 --> 00:39:12.370
reductions in cortisol, et cetera.

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:13.640
I will detail those.

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:16.380
So what happens when you do
get into a hot environment?

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:17.290
What are the mechanisms

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:20.090
that allow for the various
health effects of that?

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:25.020
Well, your shell, your skin, senses that.

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.240
And through the circuit
that I described earlier,

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:30.300
activates neurons in the
POA, the preoptic area,

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:32.440
which in turn activates mechanisms

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:35.150
in your autonomic nervous
system, like vasodilation.

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:36.660
So blood flow increases.

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:39.650
Plasma volume of your blood increases,

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:41.270
and stroke volume.

00:39:41.270 --> 00:39:44.600
The volume of blood that is mobilized

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:47.160
with each beat of your
heart also increases.

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:49.430
And your heart rate increases

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:53.030
to anywhere between 100
to 150 beats per minute.

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:55.410
That general constellation of effects

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:58.720
looks a lot like cardiovascular exercise.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:01.560
And in fact, for all intents and purposes,

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:03.980
it really is cardiovascular exercise,

00:40:03.980 --> 00:40:06.600
except that there isn't the mobilization

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.600
and the loading of joints and
limbs and things of that sort.

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:11.240
And of course there
are additional benefits

00:40:11.240 --> 00:40:13.070
of cardiovascular exercise

00:40:13.070 --> 00:40:16.220
that relate to impact on the ground,

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:18.830
improvements in bone density,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:40:18.830 --> 00:40:20.670
But basically your heart starts beating,

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:22.493
more blood starts circulating,

00:40:22.493 --> 00:40:25.020
your vasculature changes shape, literally,

00:40:25.020 --> 00:40:26.810
to accommodate those increases

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:29.440
in heart rate and blood volume.

00:40:29.440 --> 00:40:32.020
And you're basically getting
a cardiovascular workout

00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:35.580
in that hot environment, even
if you're just sitting down.

00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:36.980
Another set of positive effects

00:40:36.980 --> 00:40:39.410
related to being in these hot environments

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:42.060
are hormone effects,

00:40:42.060 --> 00:40:44.020
shifts in the output of hormones,

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:45.690
both from or your adrenals

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:48.080
and possibly from the testes and ovaries

00:40:48.080 --> 00:40:49.930
and even within the brain.

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:52.650
One of the more striking examples of that

00:40:52.650 --> 00:40:56.670
comes from a study that
was published in 2021.

00:40:56.670 --> 00:40:58.257
The title of the study is

00:40:58.257 --> 00:41:00.607
"Endocrine Effects of
Repeated Hot Thermal Stress

00:41:00.607 --> 00:41:03.660
"and Cold Water Immersion
in Young Adult Men."

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:06.320
And indeed, this study was, in
this case, just done on men.

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:08.821
I'll just briefly describe
the protocol they used.

00:41:08.821 --> 00:41:11.800
They had these men attend
four sauna sessions

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:13.430
of 12 minutes each.

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:15.820
So again, well within that
range of five to 20 minutes,

00:41:15.820 --> 00:41:17.360
12 minutes.

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:18.780
The temperature of those saunas

00:41:18.780 --> 00:41:21.490
was 90 to 91 degrees Celsius.

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:23.855
So I'll just quickly do the calculation,

00:41:23.855 --> 00:41:25.300
admittedly, not in my head.

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:30.300
That's 194 degrees Fahrenheit,
and they did that four times.

00:41:30.625 --> 00:41:33.860
Afterwards they had a
six-minute cool down break

00:41:33.860 --> 00:41:36.630
during which they did
get into some cool water

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:39.929
or cold water of about
10 degrees, which is,

00:41:39.929 --> 00:41:43.920
10 degrees Celsius is
50 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:46.060
And then they measured
hormones at various times

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:48.830
throughout this study,
before, during, and after.

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:50.130
They looked at testosterone,

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:54.020
they looked at DHEA, which
is in the androgen pathway.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:57.180
They looked at prolactin
and they looked at cortisol.

00:41:57.180 --> 00:41:59.210
The significant effects of the protocol

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:02.010
that I just described were on cortisol,

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:04.015
a so-called stress hormone.

00:42:04.015 --> 00:42:06.260
So-called because when
we are very stressed

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:07.300
for long periods of time,

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:09.780
cortisol levels tend to
increase dramatically.

00:42:09.780 --> 00:42:14.480
But I should point out that a
increase in cortisol each day

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:15.860
right about the time of waking,

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.650
and specifically right
about the time of waking,

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:19.950
is actually beneficial for
our alertness and our energy.

00:42:19.950 --> 00:42:23.310
So having some increasing
cortisol every 24 hours

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:26.500
is a good thing, provided
it happens early in the day.

00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:28.520
Late day increases in cortisol

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:30.330
are associated with depression.

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:33.081
That's been shown by studies
at Stanford and elsewhere.

00:42:33.081 --> 00:42:34.760
The major effect of this study

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:37.990
is a significant decrease
in cortisol output

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:39.023
in these subjects.

00:42:40.030 --> 00:42:41.810
I think this is really
interesting and important,

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:44.300
because many people suffer from acute,

00:42:44.300 --> 00:42:46.380
meaning immediate, and longterm stress,

00:42:46.380 --> 00:42:49.210
and are looking for ways
to control their stress.

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:51.570
Controlling your cortisol is tricky.

00:42:51.570 --> 00:42:52.800
In the episode on stress,

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:54.850
I talked about supplements
such ashwagandha

00:42:54.850 --> 00:42:56.950
that can be used to limit cortisol,

00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:59.150
but you have to be careful
not to use ashwagandha

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:00.930
for extended periods of time,

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:02.350
meaning for longer than two weeks,

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:04.410
because you can get into other issues.

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:06.050
I talk about breath work protocols

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:08.030
that can allow you to clamp or reduce

00:43:08.030 --> 00:43:10.010
the stress response in real time.

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:11.610
Again, see that episode for those,

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:13.780
but many people are overworked.

00:43:13.780 --> 00:43:14.613
They're overstressed.

00:43:14.613 --> 00:43:16.680
They're, for one reason or another,

00:43:16.680 --> 00:43:19.300
they're subjected to
many too many stressors

00:43:19.300 --> 00:43:22.440
or their level of stress
resilience isn't high enough

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:25.490
to keep their cortisol levels
clamped at a healthy level.

00:43:25.490 --> 00:43:29.060
So the protocol I described
of 12 minute exposures

00:43:29.060 --> 00:43:30.810
to 90 degree environment,

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:33.210
that's again, 90 degree Celsius,

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:36.160
followed by a six-minute cool down break

00:43:36.160 --> 00:43:39.804
in cool water, 50 degrees
or so, that's pretty cold.

00:43:39.804 --> 00:43:42.610
I can imagine that you could
also just take a cool shower

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:44.010
or a cold shower afterwards,

00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:47.130
that had a very significant
effect on lowering cortisol.

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:48.720
So there you have a tool

00:43:48.720 --> 00:43:50.190
that's not a completely zero cost tool,

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:51.023
'cause you need to heat the water

00:43:51.023 --> 00:43:53.050
and you need to have access
to hot and cold water,

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:55.840
at least hot and cold
contrast of some sort,

00:43:55.840 --> 00:43:59.110
but it's fairly minimal
cost for most people.

00:43:59.110 --> 00:44:00.720
Especially if you start getting creative

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:03.170
about maybe taking a 12-minute jog

00:44:03.170 --> 00:44:05.090
wearing a lot of clothing if it's hot out,

00:44:05.090 --> 00:44:06.680
then getting into a cool shower.

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:08.626
You might not get the same extreme

00:44:08.626 --> 00:44:10.990
or significant reduction in cortisol

00:44:10.990 --> 00:44:13.470
that was observed here with
these very specific protocols,

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:14.760
but it's likely that you would get

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:17.040
a similar result over all.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:18.924
Now, I mentioned they did
look at these other hormones,

00:44:18.924 --> 00:44:20.340
and I'll just tell you

00:44:20.340 --> 00:44:22.820
that they did not see significant
shifts in testosterone,

00:44:22.820 --> 00:44:26.250
prolactin, DHEA, et cetera,
using this protocol.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.270
As you'll soon see, there
are other sauna protocols

00:44:28.270 --> 00:44:30.260
that can impact those other hormones.

00:44:30.260 --> 00:44:32.630
So if you're seeking to
use sauna to reduce stress,

00:44:32.630 --> 00:44:34.760
I think this is a very interesting

00:44:34.760 --> 00:44:37.320
and potentially useful
research-backed protocol.

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:39.980
And again, we will provide
a link to the paper

00:44:39.980 --> 00:44:42.460
if you'd like to read more about the data.

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:45.980
So that is one set of
biological effects on cortisol

00:44:45.980 --> 00:44:48.580
and the related protocol.

00:44:48.580 --> 00:44:50.690
What about some of the
other benefits of sauna?

00:44:50.690 --> 00:44:51.730
Well, we'll talk about those,

00:44:51.730 --> 00:44:54.030
but I want to talk about
those in the context

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:56.160
of the underlying mechanisms,

00:44:56.160 --> 00:44:58.170
because if you understand
those underlying mechanisms,

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:00.840
you can really tailor your sauna protocols

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:02.650
for your particular needs.

00:45:02.650 --> 00:45:04.890
One of the more dramatic
and important effects

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:08.864
of going into a hot environment
for some period of time

00:45:08.864 --> 00:45:13.320
is the activation of so-called
heat shock proteins, or HSPs.

00:45:13.320 --> 00:45:15.740
Heat shock proteins are
a protective mechanism

00:45:15.740 --> 00:45:19.840
in your brain and body to rescue proteins

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.920
that would otherwise misfold.

00:45:21.920 --> 00:45:22.753
What do I mean by this?

00:45:22.753 --> 00:45:25.840
Well, most of you're
familiar with the fact that

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:27.650
if you have protein in the kitchen,

00:45:27.650 --> 00:45:29.940
like a steak or a piece of
chicken or a piece of fish,

00:45:29.940 --> 00:45:33.720
and you heat it up, it
changes it's texture.

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:35.684
Raw meat is different than cooked meat,

00:45:35.684 --> 00:45:37.693
to be quite blunt about it.

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:42.650
Heat changes the quality of proteins,

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:43.910
not just in terms of how they taste,

00:45:43.910 --> 00:45:46.660
but the way in which they are configured.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:49.060
It changes it right down
at the molecular level.

00:45:49.990 --> 00:45:53.530
When your body goes through
changes in temperature each day,

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:54.740
and we'll talk about those changes,

00:45:54.740 --> 00:45:57.960
but in response to hot
environments or cold environments,

00:45:57.960 --> 00:46:01.780
heat shock proteins are
deployed to go and rescue

00:46:01.780 --> 00:46:04.110
and prevent the changes in proteins

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:06.300
that would be detrimental to your health.

00:46:06.300 --> 00:46:08.670
So at least in the short term,

00:46:08.670 --> 00:46:11.230
activating heat shock
proteins is a good thing.

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:12.630
You don't want heat shock proteins

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:14.510
to be activated for long periods of time,

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:17.280
because that gets to be
problematic for other reasons.

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:18.670
But these heat shock proteins,

00:46:18.670 --> 00:46:20.260
of which there are many varieties,

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:23.450
basically have the job of
traveling in your brain and body

00:46:23.450 --> 00:46:25.680
and making sure that cells

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:28.440
that contain proteins that are misfolding

00:46:28.440 --> 00:46:32.350
because they got heated up
too much, don't misfold,

00:46:32.350 --> 00:46:35.040
and they also, sort of
a protective mechanism,

00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:37.400
making sure that proteins within the cells

00:46:37.400 --> 00:46:41.310
of your brain and body don't
fold in the wrong ways.

00:46:41.310 --> 00:46:43.520
Again, I'm describing this
in very general terms,

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:47.460
but it's well established in
animal models and in humans,

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:50.740
that sauna exposure of the
sort that I described earlier

00:46:50.740 --> 00:46:52.790
activates these heat shock proteins.

00:46:52.790 --> 00:46:54.760
There's some interesting
studies that were carried out

00:46:54.760 --> 00:46:57.880
in animal models that really nicely

00:46:57.880 --> 00:47:01.100
mechanistically support the
role of heat shock proteins

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:04.360
in some of the benefits of
deliberate heat exposure.

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:06.970
Some of these studies were done in flies,

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:08.350
meaning Drosophila, fruit flies,

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:10.140
'cause there are great model organism,

00:47:10.140 --> 00:47:13.370
because you can delete
genes or add genes easily.

00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:14.660
Other studies have been done in mice.

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:17.440
And now there are also studies
being carried out in humans,

00:47:17.440 --> 00:47:18.690
and I will talk about those.

00:47:18.690 --> 00:47:20.590
One of the more dramatic examples

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:22.580
that's always touted in this field

00:47:22.580 --> 00:47:25.490
of deliberate heat exposure
as it relates to longevity

00:47:25.490 --> 00:47:29.240
is that if they expose these
flies, these fruit flies

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:34.160
to 70 minutes of a heat
stimulus that would,

00:47:34.160 --> 00:47:35.430
obviously didn't kill them,

00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:37.300
but activated heat shock proteins,

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:40.030
it could extend their life by 15%

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:42.980
in a heat shock dependent way.

00:47:42.980 --> 00:47:44.960
Meaning if they made flies

00:47:44.960 --> 00:47:47.330
that didn't have these
heat shock proteins,

00:47:47.330 --> 00:47:50.410
well then they didn't see
this extension in life.

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:52.300
And this is one of the reasons
to use model organisms.

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:54.970
This is not an experiment
that you could do in people.

00:47:54.970 --> 00:47:57.780
However, there have been
interesting studies done in humans,

00:47:57.780 --> 00:48:00.510
examining some of the
downstream molecular pathways

00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:02.270
of deliberate heat exposure

00:48:02.270 --> 00:48:03.700
that point to the mechanisms

00:48:03.700 --> 00:48:05.480
by which deliberate heat exposure

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:08.340
can help protect against
different forms of mortality,

00:48:08.340 --> 00:48:11.000
improve overall, and possibly,

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:12.930
and I want to highlight possibly,

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:14.743
possibly extend life.

00:48:15.610 --> 00:48:18.750
One such mechanism
involves a genetic program

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:22.200
involving a molecule called FOXO3.

00:48:22.200 --> 00:48:24.140
FOXO3 is a very interesting molecule,

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:27.743
because it's involved
in DNA repair pathways.

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:32.650
DNA repair is part of the
process of remaining healthy.

00:48:32.650 --> 00:48:34.700
We'd all like to think that we're born,

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:36.830
and based on the genes we have,

00:48:36.830 --> 00:48:38.440
we are healthy, healthy, healthy,

00:48:38.440 --> 00:48:40.890
then eventually we age and then we die.

00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:44.140
But from the time we're
born, until the time we die,

00:48:44.140 --> 00:48:47.010
there's a constant repair
of our proteins in our cells

00:48:47.010 --> 00:48:50.000
in a modification of the genes
that are being expressed,

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:52.180
puberty being the most dramatic example.

00:48:52.180 --> 00:48:54.560
You see a kid before
puberty and after puberty,

00:48:54.560 --> 00:48:55.393
looks like a different kid,

00:48:55.393 --> 00:48:56.830
sounds like a different kid,
thinks like a different kid.

00:48:56.830 --> 00:48:59.530
In fact, it basically is
a different human being.

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:00.570
It's not just the hormones.

00:49:00.570 --> 00:49:02.960
It's that hormones
themselves have the capacity

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:05.150
to turn on and turn off certain genes,

00:49:05.150 --> 00:49:08.480
literally converting
certain tissues and cells

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:10.970
in the brain and body to do
entirely different things.

00:49:10.970 --> 00:49:12.350
So it's not just the sprouting

00:49:12.350 --> 00:49:15.250
of new aspects of our biology.

00:49:15.250 --> 00:49:17.810
It's literally the conversion
of different brain centers

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:20.123
from one function to another.

00:49:21.770 --> 00:49:22.603
That's puberty.

00:49:22.603 --> 00:49:24.120
And we'll do a whole
episode about puberty.

00:49:24.120 --> 00:49:26.080
We actually did an episode
on sexual development

00:49:26.080 --> 00:49:28.080
that talks a little bit
about those mechanisms.

00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:31.320
But the point is that
throughout our entire lifespan,

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:33.940
genes are being turned on,
genes are being turned off.

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:34.773
Genes are being turned on.

00:49:34.773 --> 00:49:35.620
Gene are being turned off.

00:49:35.620 --> 00:49:40.493
And DNA, the stuff of genes,
gets damaged in that process.

00:49:41.451 --> 00:49:46.451
FOXO3 sits upstream in a
pathway related to DNA repair,

00:49:47.120 --> 00:49:49.533
and again, clearing of
the senescent cells.

00:49:50.420 --> 00:49:52.620
Sauna exposure,

00:49:52.620 --> 00:49:55.300
in particular sauna
exposure two to three times

00:49:55.300 --> 00:49:57.740
or ideally four to seven times per week

00:49:57.740 --> 00:50:01.410
in that 80 to 100 degree Celsius range

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:05.328
has been shown to
upregulate levels of FOXO3.

00:50:05.328 --> 00:50:10.328
FOXO3 in turn upregulates
pathways related to DNA repair

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:12.820
and clearing out of these
senescent or dead cells,

00:50:12.820 --> 00:50:14.930
which is known to be important

00:50:14.930 --> 00:50:17.530
for various aspects of
maintaining cognition

00:50:17.530 --> 00:50:20.450
and other aspects of maintaining health.

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:23.510
So these are the likely
biological mechanisms

00:50:23.510 --> 00:50:27.460
for the improvements in lifespan,

00:50:27.460 --> 00:50:29.230
or rather, I should say,

00:50:29.230 --> 00:50:30.950
these are the biological mechanisms

00:50:30.950 --> 00:50:35.200
that apparently offset some
of the cardiovascular risk

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:38.720
and other forms of mortality
that were described earlier.

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:42.230
One especially interesting
thing about FOXO3,

00:50:42.230 --> 00:50:44.430
there are individuals out there

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:47.700
that have either
additional copies of FOXO3

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:52.410
or who have versions of
FOXO3 that are hyperactive,

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:53.960
so to speak,

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:58.670
those people tend to be
2.7 times more likely

00:50:58.670 --> 00:51:01.830
to live to 100 years of age or longer.

00:51:01.830 --> 00:51:03.650
So these are people that
were just naturally,

00:51:03.650 --> 00:51:06.930
and fortunately for them,
endowed with more FOXO3,

00:51:06.930 --> 00:51:08.550
more clearance of senescent cells,

00:51:08.550 --> 00:51:10.460
more DNA repair, et cetera.

00:51:10.460 --> 00:51:13.180
For the rest of us, at
least to my knowledge,

00:51:13.180 --> 00:51:17.960
I don't have one of these
health-promoting FOXO3 mutations.

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:19.710
Remember, mutations can be beneficial

00:51:19.710 --> 00:51:20.690
or they can be detrimental.

00:51:20.690 --> 00:51:23.230
This, if your goal is to live longer,

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:25.590
is a beneficial mutation.

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:27.520
If you don't have these FOXO3 mutations

00:51:27.520 --> 00:51:29.139
that allow you to be a centenarian

00:51:29.139 --> 00:51:33.778
at 2.7 times high or
likelihood than other people,

00:51:33.778 --> 00:51:35.870
deliberate heat exposure is one way

00:51:35.870 --> 00:51:38.550
that you can increase FOXO3 activity.

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:39.980
At this point in time,

00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:42.970
meaning when looking at
the research out there,

00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:45.800
it isn't clear what the
optimal sauna protocol

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:48.370
is going to be, specifically
to increase FOXO3,

00:51:48.370 --> 00:51:50.833
and that's probably
because there isn't one.

00:51:50.833 --> 00:51:52.730
There is no sauna protocol

00:51:52.730 --> 00:51:55.990
designed specifically to reduce cortisol

00:51:55.990 --> 00:51:58.860
or specifically to increase FOXO3

00:51:58.860 --> 00:52:01.760
or specifically to activate
heat shock proteins.

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:03.090
Any deliberate heat exposure

00:52:03.090 --> 00:52:06.040
is likely to impact all
of those mechanisms.

00:52:06.040 --> 00:52:07.970
Again, I encourage you to use this guide

00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:10.760
of 80 to 100 degrees Celsius

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:14.340
as your kind of bookends
for what you can tolerate

00:52:14.340 --> 00:52:18.170
and where you want to start
and eventually transition to

00:52:18.170 --> 00:52:19.870
in terms of deliberate heat exposure.

00:52:19.870 --> 00:52:22.080
And I would encourage you to use

00:52:22.080 --> 00:52:26.870
that five to 20 minutes
per session for the sauna

00:52:26.870 --> 00:52:29.740
as your rough guide of how
long to remain in this sauna.

00:52:29.740 --> 00:52:32.210
Now, there was a study
published just this last year

00:52:32.210 --> 00:52:34.900
that was mainly focused on
deliberate cold exposure.

00:52:34.900 --> 00:52:39.030
I detailed this quite extensively
in the episode on cold.

00:52:39.030 --> 00:52:41.460
This is the beautiful
work of Susanna Søberg.

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:43.980
And that study looked at
deliberate cold exposure,

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:45.880
but also sauna exposure.

00:52:45.880 --> 00:52:49.470
And that study found that 57,

00:52:49.470 --> 00:52:53.226
yes, 57 minutes per week of sauna exposure

00:52:53.226 --> 00:52:57.980
in conjunction with 11
minutes per week total

00:52:57.980 --> 00:53:00.080
of deliberate cold exposure

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:03.170
was the threshold for getting improvements

00:53:03.170 --> 00:53:06.880
in metabolism and increases in brown fat,

00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:08.810
this very active fat tissue

00:53:08.810 --> 00:53:12.190
that improves mitochondrial
function and thermogenesis,

00:53:12.190 --> 00:53:13.050
meaning heating of the body.

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:14.890
We'll talk more about brown fat later.

00:53:14.890 --> 00:53:15.770
Why do I mention this?

00:53:15.770 --> 00:53:17.610
Well, for those of you that are interested

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:19.520
in increasing metabolism,

00:53:19.520 --> 00:53:21.160
it does seem to be most beneficial

00:53:21.160 --> 00:53:25.170
to do that 11 minutes per
week of cold exposure, again,

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:27.240
divided up across two or more sessions.

00:53:27.240 --> 00:53:28.670
So it's not 11 minutes all at once,

00:53:28.670 --> 00:53:29.890
but shorter sessions,

00:53:29.890 --> 00:53:33.860
and to get 57 minutes minimum per week

00:53:33.860 --> 00:53:35.060
of sauna exposure, again,

00:53:35.060 --> 00:53:37.520
in the temperature ranges
that I've talked about here.

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:42.290
And again, it's not 57 minutes
in the sauna all at once.

00:53:42.290 --> 00:53:46.460
That's 57 minutes total per
week as the minimum threshold.

00:53:46.460 --> 00:53:49.760
So you might divide that into
three sessions of 20 minutes.

00:53:49.760 --> 00:53:53.370
And again, I don't think
57 is the magic number.

00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:55.610
It could be 60, it could be 64.

00:53:55.610 --> 00:53:57.290
It probably could be 55.

00:53:57.290 --> 00:53:59.250
Remember your biological systems

00:53:59.250 --> 00:54:00.990
are not counting things
off minute by minute,

00:54:00.990 --> 00:54:02.950
second by second, least not in most cases.

00:54:02.950 --> 00:54:04.230
So for those of you that are interested

00:54:04.230 --> 00:54:07.130
in improving metabolism,
check out the episode on cold,

00:54:07.130 --> 00:54:11.030
or just take the Søberg
Protocol, as I call it,

00:54:11.030 --> 00:54:15.170
which is 11 minutes total per
week of uncomfortably cold,

00:54:15.170 --> 00:54:16.860
but safe, cold exposure.

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:18.170
So uncomfortably cold means

00:54:18.170 --> 00:54:20.058
you really, really want
to get out of the shower

00:54:20.058 --> 00:54:22.130
or the ice bath or whatever environment,

00:54:22.130 --> 00:54:23.170
but you can stay in,

00:54:23.170 --> 00:54:25.780
11 minutes total per week
divided across a couple sessions

00:54:25.780 --> 00:54:29.690
and then 57 minutes per week, or so,

00:54:29.690 --> 00:54:32.000
of deliberate heat exposure,

00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:33.590
again, uncomfortably,

00:54:33.590 --> 00:54:35.590
but uncomfortably hot, excuse me,

00:54:35.590 --> 00:54:37.290
but safe to stay in,

00:54:37.290 --> 00:54:39.820
probably divided up across
three or more sessions.

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:41.740
Okay, so we've talked
about the use of sauna

00:54:41.740 --> 00:54:43.610
to decrease cortisol.

00:54:43.610 --> 00:54:44.670
We've talked about the use of sauna

00:54:44.670 --> 00:54:47.160
to increase heat shock proteins.

00:54:47.160 --> 00:54:48.730
We've talked about the use of sauna

00:54:48.730 --> 00:54:51.380
to increase FOXO3.

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:53.210
Now I'd like to talk
about the use of sauna

00:54:53.210 --> 00:54:55.490
to increase growth hormone.

00:54:55.490 --> 00:54:57.010
Growth hormone is a hormone

00:54:57.010 --> 00:54:59.840
that we all naturally
secrete from our pituitary,

00:54:59.840 --> 00:55:02.840
which also resides near
the roof of our mouth.

00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:06.580
The signal for the pituitary
to release growth hormone

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:10.480
arrives from neurons that
exist in the hypothalamus.

00:55:10.480 --> 00:55:13.450
So growth hormone releasing hormones,

00:55:13.450 --> 00:55:14.850
believe it or not, that's
what they're called,

00:55:14.850 --> 00:55:17.270
stimulate the release of growth hormone

00:55:17.270 --> 00:55:19.710
from the anterior pituitary gland

00:55:19.710 --> 00:55:21.230
into the general circulation,

00:55:21.230 --> 00:55:25.340
and then growth hormone
impacts metabolism and growth

00:55:25.340 --> 00:55:27.590
of cells and tissues of the body.

00:55:27.590 --> 00:55:30.570
It is responsible for
tissue repair as well.

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:31.990
And the growth spurt

00:55:31.990 --> 00:55:33.870
that everyone experiences during puberty

00:55:33.870 --> 00:55:36.189
is the consequence of growth hormone.

00:55:36.189 --> 00:55:39.010
What I'm about to describe is a study

00:55:39.010 --> 00:55:43.430
that found dramatic, really
dramatic I should say,

00:55:43.430 --> 00:55:45.210
increases in growth hormone,

00:55:45.210 --> 00:55:47.430
but I also want to emphasize
that these increases

00:55:47.430 --> 00:55:49.410
in growth hormone were not of the sort

00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:51.870
that are observed in puberty or in infants

00:55:51.870 --> 00:55:55.040
becoming adolescents or
adolescents growing into teenagers.

00:55:55.040 --> 00:55:57.100
Those levels of growth
hormone that are associated

00:55:57.100 --> 00:56:00.300
with those massive
transformations, excuse me,

00:56:00.300 --> 00:56:02.800
of body morphology, of shape,

00:56:02.800 --> 00:56:06.310
are far greater than the sorts
that I'm talking about here.

00:56:06.310 --> 00:56:09.765
And yet, as all of us age,

00:56:09.765 --> 00:56:13.090
when we go from adolescence
to our teenage years

00:56:13.090 --> 00:56:14.530
and then into a young adulthood,

00:56:14.530 --> 00:56:17.065
but then starting in our early 30s or so,

00:56:17.065 --> 00:56:19.350
the amount of growth
hormone that we secrete

00:56:19.350 --> 00:56:20.673
is greatly diminished.

00:56:21.590 --> 00:56:24.810
Normally we would release
growth hormone every night,

00:56:24.810 --> 00:56:25.670
after we go to sleep,

00:56:25.670 --> 00:56:27.670
in particular in the
early part of the night

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:31.600
when our sleep is comprised
mostly of slow-wave sleep.

00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:34.440
As we age, less growth hormone is released

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:36.500
during that slow wave sleep.

00:56:36.500 --> 00:56:37.630
There are various things

00:56:37.630 --> 00:56:39.900
that can promote the
release of growth hormone,

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:42.120
and we will talk about
some of those other things

00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:44.980
in a moment, things like low blood sugar,

00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:49.980
turns out, is a stimulus
for growth hormone release.

00:56:50.480 --> 00:56:52.730
And I don't mean hypoglycemia of the sort

00:56:52.730 --> 00:56:54.160
that makes you dizzy and want to pass out.

00:56:54.160 --> 00:56:55.729
That's bad.

00:56:55.729 --> 00:56:57.510
I mean, not having high
levels of glucose and insulin

00:56:57.510 --> 00:56:58.370
in your bloodstream.

00:56:58.370 --> 00:57:00.390
This is one of the reasons why many people

00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:04.430
are drawn to intermittent fasting
or even prolonged fasting,

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:07.440
it's because of the reported
increases in growth hormone.

00:57:07.440 --> 00:57:08.700
I'll touch on those briefly,

00:57:08.700 --> 00:57:10.100
but if you want to learn more about those

00:57:10.100 --> 00:57:13.260
and what their real impact is

00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:14.950
and the extent of growth hormone,

00:57:14.950 --> 00:57:17.180
check out the episode I did on fasting.

00:57:17.180 --> 00:57:19.320
You can find that at hubermanlab.com.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.440
Certain forms of exercise
have also been shown

00:57:21.440 --> 00:57:23.490
to stimulate growth hormone release.

00:57:23.490 --> 00:57:24.323
And in a few moments,

00:57:24.323 --> 00:57:26.810
I'll talk about how exercise
and fasting can be combined

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:30.010
or how heat can be combined with exercise

00:57:30.010 --> 00:57:31.310
or certain patterns of food intake

00:57:31.310 --> 00:57:32.620
to further increase growth hormone.

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:34.144
But before I do that,

00:57:34.144 --> 00:57:35.060
I want to review some of the data,

00:57:35.060 --> 00:57:36.650
and one study in particular,

00:57:36.650 --> 00:57:40.510
that discovered certain forms
of deliberate heat exposure

00:57:40.510 --> 00:57:44.090
using sauna can stimulate
very large increases

00:57:44.090 --> 00:57:45.440
in growth hormone output,

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:48.420
which for people in their
30s, 40s, and beyond

00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:49.420
could be very useful,

00:57:49.420 --> 00:57:50.700
and may also be useful for people

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:52.150
who are just trying to stimulate

00:57:52.150 --> 00:57:53.720
the release of more growth hormone

00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:55.910
in order to, for instance,
recover from exercise

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:58.410
or stimulate fat loss or
muscle growth or repair

00:57:58.410 --> 00:57:59.560
of a particular injury.

00:58:00.820 --> 00:58:01.700
The title of this paper

00:58:01.700 --> 00:58:04.630
is "Endocrine Effects of
Repeated Sauna Bathing."

00:58:04.630 --> 00:58:07.390
And this is a paper that
was published in 1986,

00:58:07.390 --> 00:58:08.660
which is some years ago,

00:58:08.660 --> 00:58:11.020
but nonetheless serves as a basis

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:13.084
for a lot of other studies that followed.

00:58:13.084 --> 00:58:15.434
So let me describe what
they did in this study.

00:58:16.400 --> 00:58:18.440
They used an 80 degree
Celsius environment,

00:58:18.440 --> 00:58:20.663
so that's 176 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:58:21.860 --> 00:58:25.880
and they had subjects do
the sauna for 30 minutes,

00:58:25.880 --> 00:58:27.600
four times per day.

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:30.870
So that's two hours total in one day,

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:33.510
30 minutes in the sauna, a
period of cool down rest,

00:58:33.510 --> 00:58:34.750
30 minutes in the sauna again,

00:58:34.750 --> 00:58:36.990
cool down rest, a third,
and a fourth time.

00:58:36.990 --> 00:58:41.060
So, two hours total in this
80 degree Celsius environment.

00:58:41.060 --> 00:58:42.220
So that's a lot,

00:58:42.220 --> 00:58:45.530
but what they observed was
really quite significant.

00:58:45.530 --> 00:58:47.480
So they had subjects do this protocol,

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:50.240
and I should mention they had
both male and female subjects

00:58:50.240 --> 00:58:51.730
in this study,

00:58:51.730 --> 00:58:53.620
and the entire study lasted a week.

00:58:53.620 --> 00:58:56.460
They did this two hours of
sauna exposure on day one,

00:58:56.460 --> 00:58:59.640
day three, and day seven of that week.

00:58:59.640 --> 00:59:02.430
And they measured a lot
of different hormones,

00:59:02.430 --> 00:59:05.318
cortisol, thyroid stimulating hormone,

00:59:05.318 --> 00:59:07.220
thyroid hormone itself,

00:59:07.220 --> 00:59:10.350
luteinizing hormone, and
follicle stimulating hormone,

00:59:10.350 --> 00:59:13.640
which are hormones that essentially drive

00:59:13.640 --> 00:59:15.070
the production of other hormones.

00:59:15.070 --> 00:59:16.570
We won't get into that too deeply,

00:59:16.570 --> 00:59:18.320
but if you'd like to learn about FSH,

00:59:18.320 --> 00:59:20.210
follicle stimulating hormone,
and luteinizing hormone,

00:59:20.210 --> 00:59:22.650
please see the episode on optimizing

00:59:22.650 --> 00:59:25.490
testosterone and estrogen
at hubermanlab.com.

00:59:25.490 --> 00:59:28.660
They looked at prolactin and
they looked at growth hormone.

00:59:28.660 --> 00:59:30.090
I'll just cut to the chase

00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:32.333
and tell you the effects
on growth hormone.

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:36.000
In subjects that did this two hour a day,

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:38.860
80 degree Celsius protocol

00:59:38.860 --> 00:59:43.580
experienced 16-fold
increases in growth hormone.

00:59:43.580 --> 00:59:44.790
So they measured growth hormone

00:59:44.790 --> 00:59:47.010
before the sauna and after the sauna

00:59:47.010 --> 00:59:49.730
and growth hormone levels went up 16 fold,

00:59:49.730 --> 00:59:52.640
which is obviously an enormous,

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:55.993
and it turns out statistically
significant, effect.

00:59:56.890 --> 00:59:59.415
Now, one important caveat here.

00:59:59.415 --> 01:00:02.220
Remember earlier when I talked about

01:00:02.220 --> 01:00:04.130
people who did sauna once a week

01:00:04.130 --> 01:00:05.670
versus two to three times a week

01:00:05.670 --> 01:00:07.210
versus four to seven times a week,

01:00:07.210 --> 01:00:09.400
and the more often people did sauna,

01:00:09.400 --> 01:00:12.190
the less likely they were to
die of cardiovascular events

01:00:12.190 --> 01:00:13.880
or other things of that sort?

01:00:13.880 --> 01:00:15.320
Well, in this case,

01:00:15.320 --> 01:00:18.240
the effects of sauna
exposure on growth hormone

01:00:18.240 --> 01:00:21.440
actually went down the more often

01:00:21.440 --> 01:00:24.350
that people did this
deliberate heat exposure.

01:00:24.350 --> 01:00:25.183
So as I mentioned,

01:00:25.183 --> 01:00:26.710
they did this two hour a day

01:00:26.710 --> 01:00:28.680
divided into 30 minute sessions protocol

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:33.050
on day one, day three,
and day seven of a week.

01:00:33.050 --> 01:00:35.170
And what they found was on day one,

01:00:35.170 --> 01:00:38.350
there was a 16-fold
increase in growth hormone.

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:39.700
On day three, however,

01:00:39.700 --> 01:00:42.820
there was still a significant
effect on growth hormone

01:00:42.820 --> 01:00:44.770
as compared to before sauna,

01:00:44.770 --> 01:00:49.100
but that effect was
basically cut by two thirds.

01:00:49.100 --> 01:00:52.040
So now instead of getting
a 16-fold increase,

01:00:52.040 --> 01:00:54.990
it was more like a three
or four-fold increase,

01:00:54.990 --> 01:00:56.800
which is still a huge increase,

01:00:56.800 --> 01:00:59.710
but not as great as the
increase observed on day one.

01:00:59.710 --> 01:01:02.130
And then on day seven, there tended to be

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:04.723
a two, maybe a threefold increase,

01:01:04.723 --> 01:01:08.033
but not as great as the
one observed on day one.

01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:10.103
What does this mean?

01:01:10.103 --> 01:01:11.100
And why does this happen?

01:01:11.100 --> 01:01:15.460
Well, the reason this
happens is because heat,

01:01:15.460 --> 01:01:20.460
just like cold, is a shock
or a stressor to the system.

01:01:20.856 --> 01:01:22.610
In the context of cold,

01:01:22.610 --> 01:01:25.020
if you get into a very cold ice bath,

01:01:25.020 --> 01:01:29.580
for instance, a five-degree
ice bath, even for 20 seconds,

01:01:29.580 --> 01:01:33.630
it's known to increase
norepinephrine 200%.

01:01:33.630 --> 01:01:35.550
It can double the amount of norepinephrine

01:01:35.550 --> 01:01:37.690
that you suddenly release
into your brain and body,

01:01:37.690 --> 01:01:39.290
which actually can have
some positive effects.

01:01:39.290 --> 01:01:41.430
I'll talk about those in a little bit,

01:01:41.430 --> 01:01:43.850
but if you were to do that every day,

01:01:43.850 --> 01:01:45.840
you would become cold adapted.

01:01:45.840 --> 01:01:50.280
This circuit that compares
the shelling core of your body

01:01:50.280 --> 01:01:53.863
would adjust in ways that
it could either predict

01:01:53.863 --> 01:01:55.240
that cold stimulus,

01:01:55.240 --> 01:01:58.650
or more likely to create
some thermogenetic mechanisms

01:01:58.650 --> 01:02:02.105
in preparation for that cold exposure.

01:02:02.105 --> 01:02:03.850
This is why, for instance,

01:02:03.850 --> 01:02:05.520
people that use deliberate cold exposure

01:02:05.520 --> 01:02:08.140
to try and increase
lipolysis, the burning of fat,

01:02:08.140 --> 01:02:09.760
oftentimes will get results for a while,

01:02:09.760 --> 01:02:11.330
but then if they're doing it a lot, a lot,

01:02:11.330 --> 01:02:13.160
they stop getting those effects.

01:02:13.160 --> 01:02:15.380
I talk a lot about
avoiding cold adaptation,

01:02:15.380 --> 01:02:18.450
if that's your goal,
in the episode on cold,

01:02:18.450 --> 01:02:20.870
but similar mechanisms are at play here.

01:02:20.870 --> 01:02:22.280
So we have to imagine that

01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:24.290
when the subjects got
into the sauna on day one,

01:02:24.290 --> 01:02:25.930
whatever pathways went

01:02:25.930 --> 01:02:28.580
from measurement of
temperature at the shell

01:02:28.580 --> 01:02:30.660
to changes in temperature at the core

01:02:30.660 --> 01:02:32.730
led to these big increases
in growth hormone,

01:02:32.730 --> 01:02:34.620
which is basically a way of
just describing the result

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:36.260
I already told you before.

01:02:36.260 --> 01:02:38.910
But the fact that that
result diminished over time

01:02:38.910 --> 01:02:41.160
either means that the
circuit was not as efficient

01:02:41.160 --> 01:02:43.730
in communicating that shift in temperature

01:02:43.730 --> 01:02:46.660
or that that shift in
temperature was of less impact

01:02:46.660 --> 01:02:49.260
because the downstream effectors

01:02:49.260 --> 01:02:51.170
were not engaged to the same extent

01:02:51.170 --> 01:02:52.890
because it wasn't as much of a shock.

01:02:52.890 --> 01:02:55.110
And I think the latter
explanation is far more likely.

01:02:55.110 --> 01:02:57.770
This is very much akin to weight training

01:02:57.770 --> 01:02:59.210
or cardiovascular exercise,

01:02:59.210 --> 01:03:01.730
where if you run up a hill
very fast, for instance,

01:03:01.730 --> 01:03:03.130
and your lungs are burning

01:03:03.130 --> 01:03:05.040
and you're heaving and breathing hard,

01:03:05.040 --> 01:03:07.870
on the first day, that's
a very painful thing.

01:03:07.870 --> 01:03:09.990
But if you do it every
day or every other day,

01:03:09.990 --> 01:03:11.570
provided you allow yourself to recover,

01:03:11.570 --> 01:03:12.750
pretty soon you're running up that hill

01:03:12.750 --> 01:03:14.450
and you're not breathing as hard.

01:03:14.450 --> 01:03:16.670
There isn't as much
burning in your muscles,

01:03:16.670 --> 01:03:18.050
et cetera, et cetera.

01:03:18.050 --> 01:03:19.400
Your body adapts.

01:03:19.400 --> 01:03:21.620
So, one of the key things to understand

01:03:21.620 --> 01:03:23.730
about the use of deliberate heat exposure

01:03:23.730 --> 01:03:25.300
is if you're going to use it

01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:27.410
in order to try and
trigger massive increases

01:03:27.410 --> 01:03:28.370
in growth hormone,

01:03:28.370 --> 01:03:29.840
you're going to need to be careful about

01:03:29.840 --> 01:03:32.440
not doing it more than,
let's say, once a week.

01:03:32.440 --> 01:03:34.450
Now, I'm extrapolating from this study.

01:03:34.450 --> 01:03:36.930
Maybe once every 10 days
would be even better,

01:03:36.930 --> 01:03:39.670
but if you start getting heat adapted,

01:03:39.670 --> 01:03:42.090
it's very unlikely that
you're going to get

01:03:42.090 --> 01:03:44.510
these massive increases in growth hormone.

01:03:44.510 --> 01:03:45.920
So I don't mean to be discouraging

01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:47.410
of using deliberate heat exposure

01:03:47.410 --> 01:03:49.690
to access growth hormone increases,

01:03:49.690 --> 01:03:52.960
but if that's your specific
goal or your main goal,

01:03:52.960 --> 01:03:55.290
then I think it's reasonable to say

01:03:55.290 --> 01:03:57.440
that you don't want to do
deliberate heat exposure,

01:03:57.440 --> 01:03:59.520
at least not of the sort
that I described here,

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:02.170
more than once a week, or
maybe even once every 10 days,

01:04:02.170 --> 01:04:03.590
and that you would want to time that

01:04:03.590 --> 01:04:06.380
to other events in your
life, maybe hard workouts,

01:04:06.380 --> 01:04:10.420
or if you are trying to push
through a fat loss barrier

01:04:10.420 --> 01:04:14.070
or simply in order to access
growth hormone at peak levels,

01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:17.210
maybe three times per month
or four times per month.

01:04:17.210 --> 01:04:19.600
If you start doing deliberate
heat exposure more often,

01:04:19.600 --> 01:04:21.710
you'll still get increases
in growth hormone,

01:04:21.710 --> 01:04:24.170
but they are not going
to be nearly as large

01:04:24.170 --> 01:04:25.980
as the increases in growth hormone

01:04:25.980 --> 01:04:29.450
that you're going to experience
if you shock your system

01:04:29.450 --> 01:04:32.660
with deliberate heat exposure
every once in a while.

01:04:32.660 --> 01:04:34.560
An important way to frame this

01:04:34.560 --> 01:04:36.200
is actually in the context of cold.

01:04:36.200 --> 01:04:37.380
And while you might say, wait,

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:39.880
this is an episode on heat
and heating, not cold,

01:04:39.880 --> 01:04:42.010
you really can't have a
conversation about heat and heating

01:04:42.010 --> 01:04:43.680
without talking about cold.

01:04:43.680 --> 01:04:45.830
Because, as I mentioned earlier,

01:04:45.830 --> 01:04:48.450
if you cool the outside
of your body, the shell,

01:04:48.450 --> 01:04:50.070
you're actually heating up your body.

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:52.407
In fact, the circuits that
control heating of the body

01:04:52.407 --> 01:04:55.140
and that control cooling of the body,

01:04:55.140 --> 01:04:57.380
for instance, the activation
of things like shiver

01:04:57.380 --> 01:04:59.920
or fat loss in response
to cold and shiver,

01:04:59.920 --> 01:05:02.190
those are also controlled
by the preoptic area

01:05:02.190 --> 01:05:03.624
of the hypothalamus.

01:05:03.624 --> 01:05:06.950
So we can take a step back
and start to think about

01:05:06.950 --> 01:05:08.380
what it would take to design

01:05:08.380 --> 01:05:12.110
the optimal protocol for
deliberate heat exposure

01:05:12.110 --> 01:05:13.340
by looking at cold,

01:05:13.340 --> 01:05:14.490
and here's what I mean.

01:05:15.460 --> 01:05:16.510
There have been beautiful studies

01:05:16.510 --> 01:05:20.530
showing that if people get
into a very cold body of water,

01:05:20.530 --> 01:05:22.410
four degrees Celsius for 20 seconds.

01:05:22.410 --> 01:05:23.243
As I mentioned earlier,

01:05:23.243 --> 01:05:26.740
that will cause the a 200 to 300% increase

01:05:26.740 --> 01:05:28.090
in norepinephrine.

01:05:28.090 --> 01:05:30.430
Norepinephrine is also
called noradrenaline.

01:05:30.430 --> 01:05:33.090
And norepinephrine and other
so-called catacholamines

01:05:33.090 --> 01:05:35.560
like dopamine increased dramatically

01:05:35.560 --> 01:05:37.940
in this very brief cold water exposure.

01:05:37.940 --> 01:05:40.290
And those increases in
norepinephrine and dopamine

01:05:40.290 --> 01:05:42.909
are known to have long-lasting effects

01:05:42.909 --> 01:05:44.840
that generally lead to improvements

01:05:44.840 --> 01:05:46.742
in mood, focus, and alertness.

01:05:46.742 --> 01:05:48.040
So they're pretty significant.

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:50.250
However, they aren't significant enough

01:05:50.250 --> 01:05:53.150
to increase metabolism
to a very high degree.

01:05:53.150 --> 01:05:55.670
Whereas, other studies have shown

01:05:55.670 --> 01:05:59.485
that if people go outside
in 16 degree Celsius weather

01:05:59.485 --> 01:06:01.300
with a proper amount,

01:06:01.300 --> 01:06:03.960
but a fairly minimum amount of clothing,

01:06:03.960 --> 01:06:07.590
you can experience even greater
increases in norepinephrine.

01:06:07.590 --> 01:06:09.960
But the time that's required in order

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:13.370
to experience those
increases is six hours at,

01:06:13.370 --> 01:06:15.400
for instance, 16 degrees Celsius.

01:06:15.400 --> 01:06:17.580
So if you have six hours a day
to be out there in the cold,

01:06:17.580 --> 01:06:19.100
or if you can turn the air conditioning on

01:06:19.100 --> 01:06:21.420
in an environment and make
it very, very cold, fine.

01:06:21.420 --> 01:06:22.900
But basically what I'm describing is that

01:06:22.900 --> 01:06:25.910
you can sort of bookend the
parameters that you can use.

01:06:25.910 --> 01:06:29.480
You can use a very brief
exposure to cold or to heat

01:06:29.480 --> 01:06:31.220
in order to stimulate heat shock proteins,

01:06:31.220 --> 01:06:32.600
growth hormone, et cetera,

01:06:32.600 --> 01:06:34.400
or you can use longer exposure

01:06:34.400 --> 01:06:38.280
in less intense versions of heat and cold.

01:06:38.280 --> 01:06:39.820
You really have to find
what's going to work for you

01:06:39.820 --> 01:06:41.450
and what you can do safely.

01:06:41.450 --> 01:06:44.240
And if you're confused
about where to start,

01:06:44.240 --> 01:06:46.610
please use the parameters
that I described earlier.

01:06:46.610 --> 01:06:48.180
First of all, check with your doctor.

01:06:48.180 --> 01:06:50.150
As always, make sure that you're somebody

01:06:50.150 --> 01:06:52.700
who can do deliberate cold
or heat exposure safely,

01:06:52.700 --> 01:06:55.750
but that 80 to 100 degrees Celsius,

01:06:55.750 --> 01:06:58.710
meaning 176 degrees Fahrenheit
to 212 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:06:58.710 --> 01:06:59.960
that I keep repeating over and over,

01:06:59.960 --> 01:07:01.180
'cause I know somebody's going to ask,

01:07:01.180 --> 01:07:03.470
even though I repeat it over
and over, which is fine,

01:07:03.470 --> 01:07:05.670
I'm delighted to keep saying it,

01:07:05.670 --> 01:07:07.570
and to respond if someone asks again.

01:07:07.570 --> 01:07:09.500
Well, those parameters
are going to kind of

01:07:09.500 --> 01:07:10.530
bookend what you should do

01:07:10.530 --> 01:07:13.750
in terms of the intensity
of the heat stimulus.

01:07:13.750 --> 01:07:14.583
How long?

01:07:14.583 --> 01:07:16.210
Well, we heard earlier,
five to 20 minutes.

01:07:16.210 --> 01:07:19.820
Why not start with five and
then ramp it up to 10 or 15?

01:07:19.820 --> 01:07:21.860
And then if you're feeling really bold

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:23.530
and you really want to
crank out growth hormone,

01:07:23.530 --> 01:07:24.363
well, then you could do

01:07:24.363 --> 01:07:26.900
that 30 minute four
times in one day stimulus

01:07:26.900 --> 01:07:28.030
every once in a while.

01:07:28.030 --> 01:07:29.010
So you have to really figure out

01:07:29.010 --> 01:07:31.940
what you're using heat exposure for.

01:07:31.940 --> 01:07:34.040
This is one of the reasons
why when people say,

01:07:34.040 --> 01:07:36.250
is it better to get in a
wet sauna or dry sauna?

01:07:36.250 --> 01:07:38.200
What's the optimal temperature?

01:07:38.200 --> 01:07:39.530
Is it better to take a hot shower

01:07:39.530 --> 01:07:40.900
or a hot bath or a hot tub?

01:07:40.900 --> 01:07:42.050
To be completely honest,

01:07:42.050 --> 01:07:44.680
it depends on what you're going
to be able to do regularly,

01:07:44.680 --> 01:07:46.130
whether or not you want
to do it regularly,

01:07:46.130 --> 01:07:47.590
and what your specific goals are.

01:07:47.590 --> 01:07:48.790
So the purpose of this episode

01:07:48.790 --> 01:07:51.010
is really to arm you with
the underlying mechanisms

01:07:51.010 --> 01:07:52.960
and to arm you with the general parameters

01:07:52.960 --> 01:07:53.890
that are going to allow you

01:07:53.890 --> 01:07:55.730
to access the results that you're seeking.

01:07:55.730 --> 01:07:58.240
For what it's worth, I
personally use a protocol,

01:07:58.240 --> 01:08:00.160
and I've been using a
protocol for a long time,

01:08:00.160 --> 01:08:02.120
that involves trying,

01:08:02.120 --> 01:08:04.470
meaning I accomplish
this most weeks, not all,

01:08:04.470 --> 01:08:06.390
trying to get into a sauna

01:08:06.390 --> 01:08:10.140
for three 20-minute sessions every week.

01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:12.860
I use a dry sauna, so
it's not a steam room.

01:08:12.860 --> 01:08:14.410
If I don't have access to it,

01:08:14.410 --> 01:08:16.210
I might take a hot bath
or something of that sort.

01:08:16.210 --> 01:08:17.060
But in general,

01:08:17.060 --> 01:08:19.343
I just stick to doing the
sauna three times a week.

01:08:19.343 --> 01:08:23.400
And I generally will do
that either after a workout,

01:08:23.400 --> 01:08:26.120
either a cardiovascular
workout or a weight workout,

01:08:26.120 --> 01:08:28.290
or I will do it later in the evening.

01:08:28.290 --> 01:08:29.910
Why later in the evening?

01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:33.140
Well, it has to do with the
circadian shifts in temperature

01:08:33.140 --> 01:08:34.810
that we all experience.

01:08:34.810 --> 01:08:37.620
Talked a lot about this
in the circadian episodes

01:08:37.620 --> 01:08:38.850
and the episodes related to sleep.

01:08:38.850 --> 01:08:41.163
But in a nutshell, here's how it works.

01:08:42.330 --> 01:08:43.780
Every early morning,

01:08:43.780 --> 01:08:47.600
about two hours before
your typical wake up time,

01:08:47.600 --> 01:08:51.003
your body temperature is
at its all-time lowest.

01:08:51.003 --> 01:08:53.240
We call that your temperature minimum.

01:08:53.240 --> 01:08:55.450
Right about waking your
body temperature increases.

01:08:55.450 --> 01:08:56.890
In fact, an increase in body temperature

01:08:56.890 --> 01:08:59.340
is part of the reason you wake up at all,

01:08:59.340 --> 01:09:01.490
unless, of course,
you're setting an alarm.

01:09:02.570 --> 01:09:03.760
Increases in body temperature

01:09:03.760 --> 01:09:06.280
are going to be one of the major things

01:09:06.280 --> 01:09:08.360
that wakes up your brain and body.

01:09:08.360 --> 01:09:10.200
Body temperature will tend to continue

01:09:10.200 --> 01:09:11.630
to increase through the morning.

01:09:11.630 --> 01:09:13.020
You'll get that increase in cortisol.

01:09:13.020 --> 01:09:14.940
That's a healthy increase in cortisol.

01:09:14.940 --> 01:09:17.030
Body tempera will increase
into the afternoon,

01:09:17.030 --> 01:09:20.250
and then we'll start to
drop in the later afternoon.

01:09:20.250 --> 01:09:22.200
This general contour can be shifted

01:09:22.200 --> 01:09:24.570
by whether or not you
exercise, how often you eat,

01:09:24.570 --> 01:09:27.080
because of the so-called
thermogenetic effects of food.

01:09:27.080 --> 01:09:29.100
That is, every time you eat
there's a slight increase

01:09:29.100 --> 01:09:30.630
in body temperature and metabolism,

01:09:30.630 --> 01:09:33.000
but it's not really that significant

01:09:33.000 --> 01:09:35.600
to throw off this general
contour and rhythm,

01:09:35.600 --> 01:09:38.020
but toward the afternoon
around four or five o'clock,

01:09:38.020 --> 01:09:39.780
most days, depending on time of year,

01:09:39.780 --> 01:09:41.170
your body temperature will peak,

01:09:41.170 --> 01:09:42.700
and then it will start to drop.

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:45.280
And as your body temperature
drops by one to three degrees,

01:09:45.280 --> 01:09:47.100
and here I'm referring to
your core body temperature,

01:09:47.100 --> 01:09:49.320
not your shell body temperature,

01:09:49.320 --> 01:09:50.730
you will start to get sleepy

01:09:50.730 --> 01:09:52.590
and to transition into sleep

01:09:52.590 --> 01:09:54.966
and to maintain sleep
throughout the night.

01:09:54.966 --> 01:09:57.110
Your body temperature will remain low

01:09:57.110 --> 01:09:58.270
until you hit that temperature minimum,

01:09:58.270 --> 01:10:00.610
and then it'll start to come up again.

01:10:00.610 --> 01:10:03.730
What that means is that when you decide

01:10:03.730 --> 01:10:06.373
to do sauna, or cold
exposure for that matter,

01:10:06.373 --> 01:10:07.670
it's going to be important.

01:10:07.670 --> 01:10:08.503
Why?

01:10:08.503 --> 01:10:09.830
Well, as I mentioned earlier,

01:10:09.830 --> 01:10:11.900
if you were to make the
surface of your body cold,

01:10:11.900 --> 01:10:14.620
at least in the immediate
period after that,

01:10:14.620 --> 01:10:16.930
your body temperature will increase.

01:10:16.930 --> 01:10:20.360
So for those of you that are
challenged in getting to sleep

01:10:20.360 --> 01:10:21.910
and are still working on your sleep,

01:10:21.910 --> 01:10:23.200
remember sleep is the foundation

01:10:23.200 --> 01:10:25.690
of all mental and physical
health and optimal performance,

01:10:25.690 --> 01:10:28.040
you should try to get really quality sleep

01:10:28.040 --> 01:10:30.470
of sufficient duration,
at least 80% of nights.

01:10:30.470 --> 01:10:32.900
That should be an ongoing
goal throughout your lifespan

01:10:32.900 --> 01:10:34.573
for a huge number of reasons.

01:10:35.650 --> 01:10:36.990
Watch the master sleep episode

01:10:36.990 --> 01:10:38.320
if you'd like to hear
are more of those reasons

01:10:38.320 --> 01:10:40.370
and the mechanisms to make
sure that you do that.

01:10:40.370 --> 01:10:41.763
But in any event,

01:10:42.740 --> 01:10:44.550
cold exposure late in the evening

01:10:44.550 --> 01:10:48.310
will start to increase your
body temperature again.

01:10:48.310 --> 01:10:50.480
And that can make it hard for
some people to fall asleep.

01:10:50.480 --> 01:10:52.400
Now, if you're very, very tired,

01:10:52.400 --> 01:10:54.020
because you've been working
hard or training hard

01:10:54.020 --> 01:10:55.360
or both throughout the day,

01:10:55.360 --> 01:10:57.550
might not throw off your sleep so much.

01:10:57.550 --> 01:10:58.383
I've gone through bouts

01:10:58.383 --> 01:11:00.550
where I'm just so, so busy
from morning till night,

01:11:00.550 --> 01:11:02.470
that the only time I can
get into the ice bath

01:11:02.470 --> 01:11:03.900
or the cold shower is late in the evening

01:11:03.900 --> 01:11:05.850
and I have no trouble sleeping after that.

01:11:05.850 --> 01:11:07.270
However, if you have trouble sleeping,

01:11:07.270 --> 01:11:10.100
I would recommend doing the
cold exposure early in the day

01:11:10.100 --> 01:11:12.380
to match that natural heating,

01:11:12.380 --> 01:11:15.340
that natural increase in body temperature

01:11:15.340 --> 01:11:18.723
that occurs across the 24-hour
so-called circadian rhythm.

01:11:19.960 --> 01:11:23.123
Similarly, if you're going to
use deliberate heat exposure,

01:11:24.530 --> 01:11:27.210
you'd be wise to do that later in the day.

01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:28.840
You'd be wise to do it later in the day

01:11:28.840 --> 01:11:31.590
because when you get
into a warm environment,

01:11:31.590 --> 01:11:34.300
sure, the surface of your
body, the shell, heats up,

01:11:34.300 --> 01:11:36.610
the core of your body heats up,

01:11:36.610 --> 01:11:39.560
but then it also activates
cooling mechanisms

01:11:39.560 --> 01:11:41.320
through the preoptic area,

01:11:41.320 --> 01:11:43.830
and when you get out of
that hot environment,

01:11:43.830 --> 01:11:47.700
sauna or otherwise, your body
will continue to cool down.

01:11:47.700 --> 01:11:49.660
And so many people find
that if they do sauna

01:11:49.660 --> 01:11:50.950
in the later half of the day,

01:11:50.950 --> 01:11:53.070
or even just before sleep

01:11:53.070 --> 01:11:55.865
and then take a warmish shower afterwards,

01:11:55.865 --> 01:11:57.880
then they find it easier to fall asleep.

01:11:57.880 --> 01:11:58.713
And that makes sense

01:11:58.713 --> 01:12:01.210
because their body
temperature is dropping.

01:12:01.210 --> 01:12:03.830
And in fact, if your
goal is to really promote

01:12:03.830 --> 01:12:05.750
the maximum amount of
growth hormone release,

01:12:05.750 --> 01:12:07.690
that's also going to be the
best time of day to do it,

01:12:07.690 --> 01:12:08.950
especially if you haven't eaten

01:12:08.950 --> 01:12:11.860
in the two hours before sleep.

01:12:11.860 --> 01:12:13.950
So if you're really going
for growth hormone release,

01:12:13.950 --> 01:12:15.257
you're really trying to optimize sleep,

01:12:15.257 --> 01:12:16.960
and the two things are actually linked

01:12:16.960 --> 01:12:18.260
because of the release of growth hormone

01:12:18.260 --> 01:12:21.140
that happens from the pituitary
in the early nights sleep,

01:12:21.140 --> 01:12:23.660
well, then you would be
wise to do your sauna

01:12:23.660 --> 01:12:25.900
maybe once or maybe twice a week

01:12:25.900 --> 01:12:28.220
in the evening or at nighttime,

01:12:28.220 --> 01:12:30.710
then taking a warm or
cool shower just briefly,

01:12:30.710 --> 01:12:33.550
just enough to kind of rinse off
all the sweat from the sauna,

01:12:33.550 --> 01:12:35.640
and then get ready for sleep.

01:12:35.640 --> 01:12:38.070
And to do that, not necessarily fasted,

01:12:38.070 --> 01:12:39.860
but to try and keep your levels

01:12:39.860 --> 01:12:42.570
of glucose and insulin somewhat
low in your bloodstream.

01:12:42.570 --> 01:12:46.580
The reason I say that is that
having elevated blood glucose

01:12:46.580 --> 01:12:49.720
and or insulin tends to blunt

01:12:49.720 --> 01:12:51.480
or reduce growth hormone release,

01:12:51.480 --> 01:12:54.500
and that's true for any
number of different stimuli

01:12:54.500 --> 01:12:57.930
including exercise and including sauna.

01:12:57.930 --> 01:12:59.240
So there's a really nice study on this

01:12:59.240 --> 01:13:00.770
that I can point you to,

01:13:00.770 --> 01:13:03.110
is this study that was
published in the journal Stress.

01:13:03.110 --> 01:13:04.600
Literally, that's the name of the journal.

01:13:04.600 --> 01:13:06.160
I love it when journals have these names

01:13:06.160 --> 01:13:08.370
like Pain or Stress.

01:13:08.370 --> 01:13:11.160
I find that somewhat amusing
for reasons that escape me,

01:13:11.160 --> 01:13:13.080
but nonetheless, amuse me.

01:13:13.080 --> 01:13:15.047
The title of this study is
"Growth Hormone Response

01:13:15.047 --> 01:13:18.307
"to Different Consecutive
Stress Stimuli in Healthy Men.

01:13:18.307 --> 01:13:19.300
"Is There Any Difference?"

01:13:19.300 --> 01:13:21.310
And I don't want to go into
all the details of the study,

01:13:21.310 --> 01:13:23.440
because it's pretty
extensive and complicated,

01:13:23.440 --> 01:13:24.940
but basically what they
did is they had people

01:13:24.940 --> 01:13:28.580
do sauna and then gave them a drug

01:13:28.580 --> 01:13:30.620
or a condition of having low,

01:13:30.620 --> 01:13:33.260
not dangerously low, but low blood sugar.

01:13:33.260 --> 01:13:34.640
Or they had them in a condition

01:13:34.640 --> 01:13:37.350
where they had low blood
sugar and then did sauna.

01:13:37.350 --> 01:13:39.310
Or they had them do an exercise protocol

01:13:39.310 --> 01:13:42.910
that led them to increased growth hormone

01:13:42.910 --> 01:13:44.280
and then had them do low blood sugar.

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:47.200
Basically mixing and
matching the various stimuli

01:13:47.200 --> 01:13:49.020
that could increase growth hormone.

01:13:49.020 --> 01:13:51.710
And what they found was
very straightforward.

01:13:51.710 --> 01:13:54.380
What they found was that doing sauna once

01:13:54.380 --> 01:13:56.400
and then waiting some period of time

01:13:56.400 --> 01:13:58.160
and then later that day doing sauna again,

01:13:58.160 --> 01:14:01.130
they didn't see the same increase

01:14:01.130 --> 01:14:03.600
in growth hormone both times.

01:14:03.600 --> 01:14:05.300
First they got a big
increase in growth hormone

01:14:05.300 --> 01:14:07.600
and then less if they did sauna again.

01:14:07.600 --> 01:14:09.993
If they had people do
exercise and then sauna,

01:14:09.993 --> 01:14:12.870
what they found was exercise
could stimulate growth hormone,

01:14:12.870 --> 01:14:14.810
but then following it with sauna

01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:18.560
did not allow you to get
twice as much growth hormone.

01:14:18.560 --> 01:14:21.040
In general, anytime you
release growth hormone,

01:14:21.040 --> 01:14:22.480
you reduce the likelihood

01:14:22.480 --> 01:14:25.200
that you're going to
release growth hormone again

01:14:25.200 --> 01:14:26.270
later that day.

01:14:26.270 --> 01:14:28.270
And this partially explains
that earlier study,

01:14:28.270 --> 01:14:31.390
where if people did this growth
hormone promoting protocol

01:14:31.390 --> 01:14:33.010
on day one, but then on day three

01:14:33.010 --> 01:14:34.380
they didn't see quite as big an effect,

01:14:34.380 --> 01:14:36.930
and on day seven they didn't
see quite as big effect.

01:14:36.930 --> 01:14:38.761
All it basically boils down to is that

01:14:38.761 --> 01:14:40.620
if you really want to crank out

01:14:40.620 --> 01:14:43.097
the most amount of growth
hormone in response to sauna,

01:14:43.097 --> 01:14:46.530
do it fasted or at least
not having ingested any food

01:14:46.530 --> 01:14:48.220
in the two or three hours before.

01:14:48.220 --> 01:14:50.420
You don't have to be deep into a fast,

01:14:50.420 --> 01:14:52.140
and the whole notion of what breaks a fast

01:14:52.140 --> 01:14:53.640
is kind of an interesting conversation,

01:14:53.640 --> 01:14:55.350
because it's contextual.

01:14:55.350 --> 01:14:57.040
Will a sip of coffee break your fast?

01:14:57.040 --> 01:14:59.100
Well, maybe probably not.

01:14:59.100 --> 01:15:01.370
Will one grain of sugar break your fast?

01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:02.203
No.

01:15:02.203 --> 01:15:05.000
Will an entire candy bar break your fast?

01:15:05.000 --> 01:15:07.510
Yes, it has to do with
where your blood glucose is

01:15:07.510 --> 01:15:09.300
when you ingest that particular food item.

01:15:09.300 --> 01:15:12.170
Not so much what that
food item, is per say.

01:15:12.170 --> 01:15:14.250
But the bottom line here
is if you want to crank out

01:15:14.250 --> 01:15:15.890
the most amount of growth hormone,

01:15:15.890 --> 01:15:17.380
wait a couple of hours after eating

01:15:17.380 --> 01:15:18.720
before getting into the sauna,

01:15:18.720 --> 01:15:21.970
or maybe do it before dinner
and then prepare dinner...

01:15:21.970 --> 01:15:23.730
Do the sauna before dinner, that is,

01:15:23.730 --> 01:15:25.560
then prepare dinner, then eat dinner,

01:15:25.560 --> 01:15:27.580
and then make sure that
you wait a few hours

01:15:27.580 --> 01:15:28.650
before going to sleep.

01:15:28.650 --> 01:15:31.430
You're going to have to arrange
your schedule accordingly.

01:15:31.430 --> 01:15:34.510
I know most people can't
arrange their schedule perfectly

01:15:34.510 --> 01:15:35.970
just to get growth hormone increases,

01:15:35.970 --> 01:15:37.440
nor do I think people should approach

01:15:37.440 --> 01:15:38.770
health protocols that way.

01:15:38.770 --> 01:15:42.180
I think for 90% of people 90% of the time,

01:15:42.180 --> 01:15:45.610
just getting into the sauna once or twice

01:15:45.610 --> 01:15:47.680
or three times a week is
going to be beneficial

01:15:47.680 --> 01:15:50.240
for the number of reasons
that I described earlier.

01:15:50.240 --> 01:15:52.360
And you don't want to obsess too much

01:15:52.360 --> 01:15:54.100
about out the exact conditions you need

01:15:54.100 --> 01:15:55.790
in order to get the greatest effect

01:15:55.790 --> 01:15:57.210
out of that sauna treatment.

01:15:57.210 --> 01:15:58.720
These are just some additional tweaks

01:15:58.720 --> 01:15:59.967
related to food intake

01:15:59.967 --> 01:16:03.400
and low level hypoglycemia and exercise,

01:16:03.400 --> 01:16:05.340
that if you wanted to leverage, you could.

01:16:05.340 --> 01:16:06.960
So if decreases in body temperature

01:16:06.960 --> 01:16:08.698
tend to aid the transition of sleep

01:16:08.698 --> 01:16:10.820
and getting out of a hot sauna

01:16:10.820 --> 01:16:13.340
tends to promote decreases in body,

01:16:13.340 --> 01:16:15.890
it makes sense why you would want to put

01:16:15.890 --> 01:16:18.240
your sauna exposure or other
deliberate heat exposure

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:19.650
in the second half of your day,

01:16:19.650 --> 01:16:21.410
and maybe even right before sleep.

01:16:21.410 --> 01:16:24.250
Now, regardless of what
time of day you do sauna

01:16:24.250 --> 01:16:25.740
or how frequently you do it,

01:16:25.740 --> 01:16:28.841
you're going to want to hydrate
after going in the sauna.

01:16:28.841 --> 01:16:31.550
When you go in the sauna, you lose water,

01:16:31.550 --> 01:16:33.040
and when you lose water,

01:16:33.040 --> 01:16:34.090
you need to replace it.

01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:34.923
Why?

01:16:34.923 --> 01:16:36.220
Well, you need water for all your cells,

01:16:36.220 --> 01:16:37.410
but you also need electrolytes.

01:16:37.410 --> 01:16:39.470
So make sure that you're
replacing the water

01:16:39.470 --> 01:16:41.180
that you lose in the sauna.

01:16:41.180 --> 01:16:43.790
Now there's no exact formula
of how much water to drink

01:16:43.790 --> 01:16:45.490
and whether or not you need electrolytes

01:16:45.490 --> 01:16:46.560
in that water or not.

01:16:46.560 --> 01:16:48.060
It's going to depend
on how much you sweat,

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:49.440
meaning how heat adapted you are.

01:16:49.440 --> 01:16:51.040
It's going to depend on how much salt

01:16:51.040 --> 01:16:52.890
you tend to excrete in your sweat.

01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:55.680
Huge amount of variation, but in general,

01:16:55.680 --> 01:16:57.670
one way to approach this
would be to make sure

01:16:57.670 --> 01:17:00.810
that you drink at least 16 ounces of water

01:17:00.810 --> 01:17:04.140
for every 10 minutes that you
happen to be in the sauna.

01:17:04.140 --> 01:17:06.900
You could do that before
and during and after,

01:17:06.900 --> 01:17:09.980
you could do it during and
after, or you could do it after.

01:17:09.980 --> 01:17:12.640
Now, there are other reasons
to do deliberate heat exposure

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:15.200
that have nothing to do
with cardiovascular effects,

01:17:15.200 --> 01:17:18.280
nothing to do with growth
hormone or anything of that sort,

01:17:18.280 --> 01:17:20.140
but rather have to do with improvements

01:17:20.140 --> 01:17:21.860
in mood and mental health.

01:17:21.860 --> 01:17:25.140
In fact, the data related to sauna

01:17:25.140 --> 01:17:26.920
and other forms of
deliberate heat exposure

01:17:26.920 --> 01:17:29.400
improving mood are very impressive,

01:17:29.400 --> 01:17:31.090
both at the mechanistic level

01:17:31.090 --> 01:17:33.740
and in terms of the longterm consequences

01:17:33.740 --> 01:17:35.280
that people experience.

01:17:35.280 --> 01:17:36.300
First of all, we need to ask,

01:17:36.300 --> 01:17:38.400
how is it that deliberate heat exposure

01:17:38.400 --> 01:17:40.230
can improve our mood and wellbeing?

01:17:40.230 --> 01:17:43.370
Well, it turns out that it
improves mood and wellbeing,

01:17:43.370 --> 01:17:47.240
but it also improves our
capacity to feel good

01:17:47.240 --> 01:17:49.070
in response to things that

01:17:49.070 --> 01:17:51.870
would ordinarily make
us feel somewhat good.

01:17:51.870 --> 01:17:53.560
Now, this is not a situation

01:17:53.560 --> 01:17:56.870
where you're going to be walking
around grinning ear to ear

01:17:56.870 --> 01:17:58.530
in response to nothing at all,

01:17:58.530 --> 01:18:00.230
simply because you went in a sauna.

01:18:00.230 --> 01:18:04.170
What I'm talking about is the
up regulation of pathways,

01:18:04.170 --> 01:18:06.850
meaning chemical pathways
in your brain and body

01:18:06.850 --> 01:18:10.090
that allow you to experience
pleasure in all its fullness.

01:18:10.090 --> 01:18:12.200
So here's how this whole
deliberate heat exposure,

01:18:12.200 --> 01:18:14.447
sauna, mood thing works.

01:18:14.447 --> 01:18:17.420
Many of you have probably
heard of endorphins.

01:18:17.420 --> 01:18:19.800
Endorphins are a category of molecules

01:18:19.800 --> 01:18:22.220
that are made naturally
in your brain and body

01:18:22.220 --> 01:18:23.900
and that are released in response

01:18:23.900 --> 01:18:26.640
to different forms of stressors.

01:18:26.640 --> 01:18:28.190
That's right, in response to stressors.

01:18:28.190 --> 01:18:30.540
So, if ever you've gone out on a long run,

01:18:30.540 --> 01:18:31.610
and at some point in that run,

01:18:31.610 --> 01:18:33.700
you feel like you're aching
and your joints hurt,

01:18:33.700 --> 01:18:36.600
or maybe you have shin splints,
and you push through that,

01:18:37.770 --> 01:18:39.580
part of the reason that you experience

01:18:39.580 --> 01:18:42.430
a lack of pain at some point, usually,

01:18:42.430 --> 01:18:46.068
or you experience a euphoria
during or after that exercise

01:18:46.068 --> 01:18:50.290
is the exercise-induced
effects on endorphin release.

01:18:50.290 --> 01:18:52.140
Or rather, to be more specific,

01:18:52.140 --> 01:18:55.750
I should say the
exercise-induced consequences

01:18:55.750 --> 01:18:56.900
on the stress system,

01:18:56.900 --> 01:18:59.800
which in turn trigger
the release of endorphin.

01:18:59.800 --> 01:19:00.633
In other words,

01:19:00.633 --> 01:19:04.006
when we experience short
term or acute stress,

01:19:04.006 --> 01:19:06.830
the endorphin system is activated.

01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:10.140
Now the endorphin system is
not just about feeling good,

01:19:10.140 --> 01:19:10.973
believe it or not.

01:19:10.973 --> 01:19:12.730
It also about feeling bad.

01:19:12.730 --> 01:19:15.020
And there are two general
categories of endorphins.

01:19:15.020 --> 01:19:17.120
The first are the ones that
you normally hear about,

01:19:17.120 --> 01:19:19.470
endorphins, things that bind for instance

01:19:19.470 --> 01:19:22.530
to receptors like the mu opioid receptor.

01:19:22.530 --> 01:19:25.590
Opioids are not just prescribed compounds

01:19:25.590 --> 01:19:28.070
or unfortunately drugs of abuse,

01:19:28.070 --> 01:19:29.500
which they are.

01:19:29.500 --> 01:19:30.760
We have this opioid crisis

01:19:30.760 --> 01:19:32.150
in the United States and elsewhere,

01:19:32.150 --> 01:19:34.700
which is a very serious and tragic thing,

01:19:34.700 --> 01:19:37.270
but we make endogenous opioids.

01:19:37.270 --> 01:19:40.810
We make endorphins that
naturally act as pain relievers

01:19:40.810 --> 01:19:43.650
and that make us feel mildly euphoric.

01:19:43.650 --> 01:19:47.170
We also make endorphins
such as dynorphine,

01:19:47.170 --> 01:19:52.170
that's D-Y-N-O-R-P-H-I-N-E, dynorphine,

01:19:52.260 --> 01:19:55.983
that actually make us feel
worse in response to stressors.

01:19:57.260 --> 01:19:59.450
When we get into a hot sauna,

01:19:59.450 --> 01:20:01.950
or a hot environment of any kind,

01:20:01.950 --> 01:20:04.880
dynorphins are liberated
in the brain and body.

01:20:04.880 --> 01:20:06.680
And I should mention
that dynorphins are made

01:20:06.680 --> 01:20:10.280
by many neurons in many
different areas of the brain.

01:20:10.280 --> 01:20:12.570
So you might think, well,
why would I want that?

01:20:12.570 --> 01:20:15.070
Why would I want to release
dynorphine into my brain and body?

01:20:15.070 --> 01:20:15.903
Well, first of all,

01:20:15.903 --> 01:20:19.260
when you get into an
uncomfortably hot situation,

01:20:19.260 --> 01:20:21.510
uncomfortably hot scenario, oh gosh,

01:20:21.510 --> 01:20:22.520
this is sounding terrible.

01:20:22.520 --> 01:20:25.390
And a deliberately hot environment

01:20:25.390 --> 01:20:27.250
that you are using to try and trigger

01:20:27.250 --> 01:20:30.350
some sort of biological
or psychological benefit,

01:20:30.350 --> 01:20:31.183
I should say,

01:20:32.380 --> 01:20:33.900
the discomfort that you feel,

01:20:33.900 --> 01:20:36.400
the desire to get out of that environment,

01:20:36.400 --> 01:20:40.364
is in part the consequence
of the release of dynorphine.

01:20:40.364 --> 01:20:42.020
It's also the consequence

01:20:42.020 --> 01:20:43.970
of the activation of that
sympathetic nervous system.

01:20:43.970 --> 01:20:46.090
Remember, the preoptic
area can communicate

01:20:46.090 --> 01:20:47.410
with the amygdala and trigger

01:20:47.410 --> 01:20:48.840
that kind of fight or flight mode,

01:20:48.840 --> 01:20:49.840
I want to get out of the sauna.

01:20:49.840 --> 01:20:51.240
This is really, really hot.

01:20:51.240 --> 01:20:53.490
But dynorphine is also liberated

01:20:53.490 --> 01:20:55.640
from a certain number of neurons.

01:20:55.640 --> 01:20:59.770
Dynorphine binds to what's
called the CAPA receptor.

01:20:59.770 --> 01:21:02.920
The CAPA receptor binds dynorphine

01:21:02.920 --> 01:21:05.130
and triggers pathways
in the brain and body

01:21:05.130 --> 01:21:07.810
that lead to agitation, to stress,

01:21:07.810 --> 01:21:10.700
and believe it or not to
a general sense of pain.

01:21:10.700 --> 01:21:13.210
This is why you want to
get out of the hot sauna,

01:21:13.210 --> 01:21:15.630
and remember if it's unsafe levels of hot,

01:21:15.630 --> 01:21:17.440
then you should get out of that sauna

01:21:17.440 --> 01:21:19.240
or other hot environment.

01:21:19.240 --> 01:21:21.270
But if you're working in a range

01:21:21.270 --> 01:21:22.170
or you're exposing yourself

01:21:22.170 --> 01:21:23.850
to a range of heat that's uncomfortable,

01:21:23.850 --> 01:21:27.100
but safe to be in,
dynorphine will be liberated

01:21:27.100 --> 01:21:30.260
from these neurons, bind
to the CAPA receptor.

01:21:30.260 --> 01:21:33.196
And as a downstream consequence of that,

01:21:33.196 --> 01:21:35.860
there will be an increase in the receptors

01:21:35.860 --> 01:21:38.870
that bind the other endorphins,

01:21:38.870 --> 01:21:40.780
the endorphins that make you feel soothed,

01:21:40.780 --> 01:21:41.950
that make you feel happy,

01:21:41.950 --> 01:21:44.530
and that make you feel mild euphoria.

01:21:44.530 --> 01:21:45.810
So there've been a
number of studies showing

01:21:45.810 --> 01:21:48.070
that initially deliberate heat exposure,

01:21:48.070 --> 01:21:49.590
by sauna or otherwise,

01:21:49.590 --> 01:21:51.250
causes the release of dynorphine.

01:21:51.250 --> 01:21:52.750
In fact, I think it's fair to say

01:21:52.750 --> 01:21:53.780
that every time we get

01:21:53.780 --> 01:21:55.940
into a hot environment
that's uncomfortable

01:21:55.940 --> 01:21:57.680
or a cold environment
that's uncomfortable,

01:21:57.680 --> 01:21:59.240
dynorphine is likely released

01:21:59.240 --> 01:22:00.890
and binding to the CAPA receptor.

01:22:02.350 --> 01:22:04.970
But over time that binding of dynorphine

01:22:04.970 --> 01:22:08.300
into the CAPA receptor
leads to downstream changes

01:22:08.300 --> 01:22:10.780
in the way that the feel good endorphins,

01:22:10.780 --> 01:22:13.700
things like endorphin binding
to the mu opioid receptor,

01:22:13.700 --> 01:22:15.890
and there are still other
feel good endorphins,

01:22:15.890 --> 01:22:17.530
so to speak.

01:22:17.530 --> 01:22:20.380
That system becomes much more efficient,

01:22:20.380 --> 01:22:22.280
such that people feel an elevation

01:22:22.280 --> 01:22:24.100
in their baseline level of mood,

01:22:24.100 --> 01:22:27.960
and when a good or
happy event comes along,

01:22:27.960 --> 01:22:31.180
they feel a heightened
level of happiness or joy

01:22:31.180 --> 01:22:34.250
or awe or improved mood
in response to that.

01:22:34.250 --> 01:22:36.810
This is not unlike the effects of caffeine

01:22:36.810 --> 01:22:39.110
on the dopamine receptor that
I've described previously.

01:22:39.110 --> 01:22:41.003
And for those of you that
aren't familiar with it,

01:22:41.003 --> 01:22:44.310
many of you drink caffeine and love it.

01:22:44.310 --> 01:22:45.390
Part of the reason you love it

01:22:45.390 --> 01:22:47.760
is because of the release
of certain neurochemicals,

01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:50.120
like norepinephrine, et cetera,

01:22:50.120 --> 01:22:51.190
the energy that it gives you.

01:22:51.190 --> 01:22:53.300
Maybe the taste, I would hope, as well,

01:22:53.300 --> 01:22:56.470
but caffeine ingestion
also causes increases

01:22:56.470 --> 01:22:59.330
in dopamine receptor
concentration and efficacy.

01:22:59.330 --> 01:23:01.930
In other words, it allows
the receptors for dopamine

01:23:01.930 --> 01:23:03.530
to work better so that

01:23:03.530 --> 01:23:05.630
for a given amount of dopamine release,

01:23:05.630 --> 01:23:08.860
you experience more
pleasure and motivation.

01:23:08.860 --> 01:23:10.160
This is a similar mechanism,

01:23:10.160 --> 01:23:11.950
but within the endorphin pathway.

01:23:11.950 --> 01:23:12.920
So what does it mean?

01:23:12.920 --> 01:23:15.410
It means that a little bit of discomfort

01:23:15.410 --> 01:23:17.430
as a consequence of
deliberate heat exposure,

01:23:17.430 --> 01:23:19.210
while in the short term,

01:23:19.210 --> 01:23:21.440
doesn't feel good, by definition,

01:23:21.440 --> 01:23:22.830
it is activating pathways

01:23:22.830 --> 01:23:27.140
that are allowing the feel
molecules and neural circuitries

01:23:27.140 --> 01:23:28.680
that exist in your brain and body

01:23:28.680 --> 01:23:31.410
to increase their efficiency,

01:23:31.410 --> 01:23:33.500
placing you in a better
position to be joyful

01:23:33.500 --> 01:23:35.230
in response to the events of life.

01:23:35.230 --> 01:23:37.080
I confess I'm very excited about the data

01:23:37.080 --> 01:23:38.900
on deliberate heat exposure

01:23:38.900 --> 01:23:41.670
and improvements in the chemical systems

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:44.140
that underlie good mood.

01:23:44.140 --> 01:23:46.360
And just to underscore this further,

01:23:46.360 --> 01:23:51.360
the dynorphine system is not
unique to heat-induced stress.

01:23:51.740 --> 01:23:54.770
In fact, there are beautiful
studies and reviews

01:23:54.770 --> 01:23:56.680
out there about the role of dynorphine

01:23:56.680 --> 01:23:58.850
in stress and depression,

01:23:58.850 --> 01:24:01.530
in stress and alcoholism,

01:24:01.530 --> 01:24:02.770
just as a brief aside,

01:24:02.770 --> 01:24:04.950
and in the future we
will do a whole episode

01:24:04.950 --> 01:24:07.330
on alcohol and alcoholism,

01:24:07.330 --> 01:24:10.070
but turns out that chronic
alcohol use and alcoholism

01:24:10.070 --> 01:24:12.950
causes changes in dopamine receptors

01:24:12.950 --> 01:24:15.810
that make it very difficult for people

01:24:15.810 --> 01:24:17.530
to achieve pleasure through things

01:24:17.530 --> 01:24:19.600
other than alcohol, and even alcohol.

01:24:19.600 --> 01:24:22.910
That's kind of the really
diabolical nature of addiction,

01:24:22.910 --> 01:24:24.930
which is the thing that
initially brings pleasure,

01:24:24.930 --> 01:24:26.390
eventually is just required

01:24:26.390 --> 01:24:28.880
to maintain baseline levels of dopamine.

01:24:28.880 --> 01:24:31.070
And I've talked before,
and Dr. Anna Lembke,

01:24:31.070 --> 01:24:33.012
when she was a guest on this podcast,

01:24:33.012 --> 01:24:36.210
talked about the pleasure pain balance

01:24:36.210 --> 01:24:38.170
that exists within the dopamine system.

01:24:38.170 --> 01:24:39.410
It is beautifully described

01:24:39.410 --> 01:24:41.410
in her book "Dopamine Nation," by the way.

01:24:41.410 --> 01:24:45.170
Excellent book I recommend to
all people, addicts or not.

01:24:45.170 --> 01:24:48.832
Well, in that context of pleasure and pain

01:24:48.832 --> 01:24:51.490
it's very clear what the
pleasure molecule is.

01:24:51.490 --> 01:24:53.840
It's actually a molecule
more related to motivation,

01:24:53.840 --> 01:24:55.214
and that's dopamine.

01:24:55.214 --> 01:24:59.650
The pain molecule, however,
appears to be dynorphine.

01:24:59.650 --> 01:25:03.440
And the fact that
dynorphine is dysregulated

01:25:03.440 --> 01:25:05.270
in stress and depression and alcoholism

01:25:05.270 --> 01:25:07.680
and the relationship between
dynorphine and dopamine

01:25:07.680 --> 01:25:10.000
is something that we should
all take very seriously.

01:25:10.000 --> 01:25:11.500
And for that reason,

01:25:11.500 --> 01:25:13.150
I'm very excited about the fact

01:25:13.150 --> 01:25:14.760
that deliberate heat exposure

01:25:14.760 --> 01:25:17.600
can leverage the dynorphine system

01:25:17.600 --> 01:25:19.820
in a short term and an acute way

01:25:19.820 --> 01:25:23.660
that allows mood to improve
after the sauna exposure.

01:25:23.660 --> 01:25:26.660
So, for those of you that
don't like heat exposure,

01:25:26.660 --> 01:25:31.270
keep in mind that a lot of
the observed positive effects

01:25:31.270 --> 01:25:33.780
on our biology relate to metabolism,

01:25:33.780 --> 01:25:36.910
cardiovascular function,
but also mental health.

01:25:36.910 --> 01:25:39.967
And along those lines,
there is a wonderful study,

01:25:39.967 --> 01:25:42.300
again, published in 2018.

01:25:42.300 --> 01:25:43.133
I don't know why.

01:25:43.133 --> 01:25:44.350
I guess 2018 was a big year

01:25:44.350 --> 01:25:47.585
for deliberate heat exposure studies.

01:25:47.585 --> 01:25:50.897
The title of this study is "Sauna Bathing

01:25:50.897 --> 01:25:52.760
"and Risk of Psychotic Disorders."

01:25:52.760 --> 01:25:55.180
And this was a prospective cohort study.

01:25:55.180 --> 01:25:57.240
Again, we'll provide a link to this study.

01:25:57.240 --> 01:25:58.190
It's a really interesting study

01:25:58.190 --> 01:26:01.290
that explored the relationship
between mental health,

01:26:01.290 --> 01:26:04.280
so people suffering from
various forms of psychoses,

01:26:04.280 --> 01:26:06.290
schizophrenia and other
forms of psychoses,

01:26:06.290 --> 01:26:08.040
and use of sauna.

01:26:08.040 --> 01:26:09.710
So essentially what this study did

01:26:09.710 --> 01:26:12.010
is they looked at a very
large number of subjects,

01:26:12.010 --> 01:26:14.130
more than 2,000 subjects,

01:26:14.130 --> 01:26:16.240
who had no history of psychotic disorders.

01:26:16.240 --> 01:26:17.770
They were classified into three groups

01:26:17.770 --> 01:26:20.260
based on their frequency of sauna use,

01:26:20.260 --> 01:26:23.140
either once a week, two to
three to times per week,

01:26:23.140 --> 01:26:24.900
or four to seven times per week.

01:26:24.900 --> 01:26:27.530
This should call to
mind that earlier study

01:26:27.530 --> 01:26:31.445
on all-risk mortality and
cardiovascular event risk.

01:26:31.445 --> 01:26:35.210
And then they explored the hazard ratio

01:26:35.210 --> 01:26:37.270
for psychosis specifically,

01:26:37.270 --> 01:26:38.950
meaning how likely it was that people

01:26:38.950 --> 01:26:40.590
would develop psychotic symptoms

01:26:40.590 --> 01:26:43.960
or full blown psychotic illness,

01:26:43.960 --> 01:26:46.010
according to their
frequency of sauna session.

01:26:46.010 --> 01:26:48.820
So, again, this isn't
causal, this is correlative.

01:26:48.820 --> 01:26:50.850
And according to the data in this study,

01:26:50.850 --> 01:26:52.250
what they concluded is that

01:26:52.250 --> 01:26:55.390
there was a strong and inverse
independent association

01:26:55.390 --> 01:26:56.900
between frequent sauna bathing

01:26:56.900 --> 01:26:58.890
and the future risk of psychotic disorders

01:26:58.890 --> 01:27:00.850
in this population.

01:27:00.850 --> 01:27:03.410
Now, this does not mean that going

01:27:03.410 --> 01:27:04.840
into a sauna seven times per week

01:27:04.840 --> 01:27:05.950
is going to prevent people

01:27:05.950 --> 01:27:08.360
from becoming schizophrenic, necessarily,

01:27:08.360 --> 01:27:10.770
or from having a psychotic
episode, necessarily.

01:27:10.770 --> 01:27:13.680
And of course, frequent sauna use will be

01:27:13.680 --> 01:27:16.840
related to other
health-promoting activities.

01:27:16.840 --> 01:27:19.250
But in this study, as
in the previous study,

01:27:19.250 --> 01:27:21.170
they went to great lengths in order to try

01:27:21.170 --> 01:27:24.360
and limit those so-called
confounding variables.

01:27:24.360 --> 01:27:26.200
Now, of course, this is just one study,

01:27:26.200 --> 01:27:28.600
and again, it's correlative, not causal,

01:27:28.600 --> 01:27:31.410
but based on the large number
of subjects they included,

01:27:31.410 --> 01:27:33.926
plus the rigor of the
statistical analysis,

01:27:33.926 --> 01:27:36.100
we're starting to see a general picture

01:27:36.100 --> 01:27:38.840
that using the sorts of sauna protocols

01:27:38.840 --> 01:27:41.246
that I've described
throughout this episode,

01:27:41.246 --> 01:27:44.000
five to 20 minutes or so,

01:27:44.000 --> 01:27:46.910
done one to seven times per week

01:27:46.910 --> 01:27:50.000
is associated with a general improvement

01:27:50.000 --> 01:27:51.320
in cardiovascular health,

01:27:51.320 --> 01:27:54.320
a general improvement in mental health,

01:27:54.320 --> 01:27:56.550
and it really points to the fact that,

01:27:56.550 --> 01:28:01.240
yes, sauna done acutely for
three or four times a day,

01:28:01.240 --> 01:28:03.560
30 minutes each session
separated by cooling,

01:28:03.560 --> 01:28:04.970
maybe getting into cold bath,

01:28:04.970 --> 01:28:08.200
sure that can potently
increase growth hormone,

01:28:08.200 --> 01:28:11.290
but done on a more regular
basis can reduce cortisol,

01:28:11.290 --> 01:28:13.990
improve heart health,
improve mental health.

01:28:13.990 --> 01:28:17.540
And for that reason, and the
fact that for most people,

01:28:17.540 --> 01:28:19.270
it is conceivable to come up with a way

01:28:19.270 --> 01:28:21.110
that you could get into
deliberate heat exposure

01:28:21.110 --> 01:28:22.930
for a minimum of cost.

01:28:22.930 --> 01:28:26.090
If it's a hot bath, or
if you had to resort to

01:28:26.090 --> 01:28:28.240
bundling up and going for
a jog, this sort of thing,

01:28:28.240 --> 01:28:31.990
or if you have access to
it, a sauna of some sort,

01:28:31.990 --> 01:28:34.960
that we're really talking about a stimulus

01:28:34.960 --> 01:28:38.510
to initiate a large number of
different biological cascades

01:28:38.510 --> 01:28:42.340
that wick out to improve multiple aspects

01:28:42.340 --> 01:28:43.620
of brain and body health.

01:28:43.620 --> 01:28:46.940
So up until now, I've been
talking about whole body heating.

01:28:46.940 --> 01:28:49.700
So for instance, putting your
whole body into the sauna,

01:28:49.700 --> 01:28:51.910
which of course is what most people do,

01:28:51.910 --> 01:28:55.030
or getting into a hot tub
or hot bath up to your neck,

01:28:55.030 --> 01:28:56.470
or in the cases where we were talking

01:28:56.470 --> 01:28:58.380
about deliberate cold exposure as a means

01:28:58.380 --> 01:29:01.780
to increase core body
temperature and metabolism,

01:29:01.780 --> 01:29:05.220
getting into an ice bath
or cold water of some sort

01:29:05.220 --> 01:29:08.200
up to your neck or into
a cold shower, et cetera.

01:29:08.200 --> 01:29:10.690
Now I'd like to talk about
deliberately heating or cooling

01:29:10.690 --> 01:29:13.010
specific parts of the body,

01:29:13.010 --> 01:29:16.310
meaning certain surface areas of your body

01:29:16.310 --> 01:29:19.510
as a means to get effects
on those particular areas,

01:29:19.510 --> 01:29:21.710
as well as at the whole body level.

01:29:21.710 --> 01:29:23.040
Numerous times throughout this episode,

01:29:23.040 --> 01:29:25.100
I've talked about the
dangers of overheating.

01:29:25.100 --> 01:29:27.860
So what should you do if you
think you or someone else

01:29:27.860 --> 01:29:30.390
is hyperthermic, is too hot?

01:29:30.390 --> 01:29:32.440
Well, if you understand just a little bit

01:29:32.440 --> 01:29:34.570
about the cooling and heating systems

01:29:34.570 --> 01:29:36.650
of your shell and core,

01:29:36.650 --> 01:29:38.620
there are some terrific
tools that you can use

01:29:38.620 --> 01:29:41.280
in order to cool off your core quickly.

01:29:41.280 --> 01:29:43.400
And remember the core consists
of the nervous system,

01:29:43.400 --> 01:29:44.800
the spinal cord, and the viscera,

01:29:44.800 --> 01:29:47.180
which are really the organs
you're trying to protect.

01:29:47.180 --> 01:29:50.980
So, being able to cool off
the core of your body quickly

01:29:50.980 --> 01:29:52.210
can be very beneficial,

01:29:52.210 --> 01:29:54.490
and in some cases, it
could even save your life.

01:29:54.490 --> 01:29:58.080
There is a way to more
quickly heat or cool the body,

01:29:58.080 --> 01:30:01.570
and that's through specific
elements of your shell,

01:30:01.570 --> 01:30:03.740
meaning particular skin surfaces.

01:30:03.740 --> 01:30:06.750
I've talked extensively about
this in the episode on cold.

01:30:06.750 --> 01:30:09.060
It was also covered in the episode

01:30:09.060 --> 01:30:10.630
with my guest, Dr. Craig Heller,

01:30:10.630 --> 01:30:12.977
from the Biology Department at Stanford.

01:30:12.977 --> 01:30:16.570
It relates to the so-called
glabrous skin surfaces

01:30:16.570 --> 01:30:18.693
on the upper half of our face,

01:30:18.693 --> 01:30:20.820
palms of our hands, and
the bottoms of our feet.

01:30:20.820 --> 01:30:23.120
And for those of you that
have heard this before

01:30:23.120 --> 01:30:25.100
I encourage you to continue
to listen nonetheless,

01:30:25.100 --> 01:30:26.900
because today I'm going to talk about

01:30:26.900 --> 01:30:30.230
specifically how to heat
the body or cool the body

01:30:30.230 --> 01:30:32.313
through these glabrous skin surfaces.

01:30:32.313 --> 01:30:35.680
Very briefly, the mechanism is as follows.

01:30:35.680 --> 01:30:37.027
The palms of our hands,
the bottoms of our feet,

01:30:37.027 --> 01:30:39.106
and the upper half of our face

01:30:39.106 --> 01:30:42.620
overly specific types of vasculature,

01:30:42.620 --> 01:30:46.570
meaning specific types
of veins and arteries

01:30:46.570 --> 01:30:48.980
that don't have capillaries between them,

01:30:48.980 --> 01:30:50.589
and as a consequence,

01:30:50.589 --> 01:30:54.004
heat and cold can move very quickly

01:30:54.004 --> 01:30:57.130
from the palms of the hands,
the bottoms of the feet,

01:30:57.130 --> 01:30:58.540
and the upper half of our face,

01:30:58.540 --> 01:31:01.620
and change our core body temperature.

01:31:01.620 --> 01:31:04.430
There's a name for these
particular vascular structures.

01:31:04.430 --> 01:31:08.200
They're called AVAs or
arteriovenous astemoses.

01:31:08.200 --> 01:31:12.080
Basically veins and arteries
interacting directly

01:31:12.080 --> 01:31:14.180
without capillaries in between,

01:31:14.180 --> 01:31:18.070
which allows cooling of
blood or heating of blood

01:31:18.070 --> 01:31:20.800
much more quickly than is possible

01:31:20.800 --> 01:31:23.460
by applying colder heat
elsewhere on the body,

01:31:23.460 --> 01:31:26.510
where capillaries intervene
between veins and arteries.

01:31:26.510 --> 01:31:28.980
These AVAs, arteriovenous anastomoses,

01:31:28.980 --> 01:31:30.450
can be leveraged to cool off

01:31:30.450 --> 01:31:32.280
your core body temperature very quickly.

01:31:32.280 --> 01:31:35.220
The key thing is to get
the palms of your hands,

01:31:35.220 --> 01:31:37.080
the bottoms of your feet, and
the upper half of your face

01:31:37.080 --> 01:31:40.570
in contact with a cold surface or fluid

01:31:40.570 --> 01:31:44.030
that is cold enough to cool the blood

01:31:44.030 --> 01:31:45.740
and the core of your body,

01:31:45.740 --> 01:31:49.274
but not so cold that
it constricts the veins

01:31:49.274 --> 01:31:51.700
just below the palms of your hands,

01:31:51.700 --> 01:31:54.120
the bottoms of your feet, or
the upper half of your face.

01:31:54.120 --> 01:31:57.700
So, not placing ice packs necessarily,

01:31:57.700 --> 01:32:00.960
but maybe placing cool towels
on the bottoms of feet,

01:32:00.960 --> 01:32:02.660
the palms of the hands, and
the upper half of the face,

01:32:02.660 --> 01:32:06.000
and as they warm up, replacing
those with other cool towels.

01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:07.300
The exact temperature will depend

01:32:07.300 --> 01:32:08.950
on how hot you happen to be.

01:32:08.950 --> 01:32:10.240
I can't know that without

01:32:10.240 --> 01:32:12.200
knowing your particular circumstances.

01:32:12.200 --> 01:32:13.740
If you'd like to learn more about

01:32:13.740 --> 01:32:16.260
how to cool off your core very quickly,

01:32:16.260 --> 01:32:18.360
and some of the details and
some of the technologies

01:32:18.360 --> 01:32:20.050
that are being developed to do that,

01:32:20.050 --> 01:32:21.890
please see the episode
I did with Craig Heller

01:32:21.890 --> 01:32:23.310
or the episode on cold.

01:32:23.310 --> 01:32:25.050
If you don't want to go to those episodes,

01:32:25.050 --> 01:32:27.210
here is a good procedure
that you could use.

01:32:27.210 --> 01:32:28.810
You could grab, for instance,

01:32:28.810 --> 01:32:32.160
a package of frozen broccoli
or frozen blueberries.

01:32:32.160 --> 01:32:33.460
If someone is really, really warm,

01:32:33.460 --> 01:32:34.890
make sure they take off
their shoes and socks,

01:32:34.890 --> 01:32:36.140
get their feet on top of those.

01:32:36.140 --> 01:32:37.841
Ideally get some into their hands as well.

01:32:37.841 --> 01:32:41.210
Get some cool compresses and
get them onto people's face.

01:32:41.210 --> 01:32:42.220
You could, of course,

01:32:42.220 --> 01:32:44.960
also put a cool compress
on the back of the neck,

01:32:44.960 --> 01:32:45.950
on the top of the head.

01:32:45.950 --> 01:32:47.610
That would be an especially good idea

01:32:47.610 --> 01:32:48.820
if someone were hyperthermic

01:32:48.820 --> 01:32:51.610
because of the way that
cooling of the brain occurs

01:32:51.610 --> 01:32:53.720
under conditions of hyperthermia.

01:32:53.720 --> 01:32:56.030
But the key point here is that

01:32:56.030 --> 01:32:58.630
just putting cold
compresses or cold materials

01:32:58.630 --> 01:33:01.010
onto somebody's torso is
not going to be as efficient

01:33:01.010 --> 01:33:02.760
as cooling those glabrous skin surfaces,

01:33:02.760 --> 01:33:04.440
the bottoms of the feet,
the palms of the hands,

01:33:04.440 --> 01:33:06.700
and the upper half of their face.

01:33:06.700 --> 01:33:09.250
Similarly, or I suppose
to be more accurate,

01:33:09.250 --> 01:33:10.470
I should say, conversely,

01:33:10.470 --> 01:33:13.120
there are times when it is desirable

01:33:13.120 --> 01:33:15.460
to heat the core of the body.

01:33:15.460 --> 01:33:16.550
And once again,

01:33:16.550 --> 01:33:18.870
just simply throwing a
hot towel over somebody

01:33:18.870 --> 01:33:21.180
is not going to be the most efficient way.

01:33:21.180 --> 01:33:24.542
If someone is hypothermic,
they're too cold,

01:33:24.542 --> 01:33:27.800
it is not a problem to
cover them with a blanket.

01:33:27.800 --> 01:33:32.800
But ideally what you do is
you use some warm object

01:33:33.340 --> 01:33:36.780
or warm fluid to warm the
bottoms of their feet,

01:33:36.780 --> 01:33:38.830
their hands, and the
upper half of their face.

01:33:38.830 --> 01:33:41.467
Of course not so warm that
you burn those skin surfaces.

01:33:41.467 --> 01:33:43.480
This has actually been examined in studies

01:33:43.480 --> 01:33:44.610
from the Heller Lab.

01:33:44.610 --> 01:33:45.800
Turns out that, for instance,

01:33:45.800 --> 01:33:47.707
to get people out of anesthesia,

01:33:47.707 --> 01:33:51.470
it is beneficial to warm
their core body temperature.

01:33:51.470 --> 01:33:52.980
And of course there is fever,

01:33:52.980 --> 01:33:55.700
which you should know
is an adaptive response.

01:33:55.700 --> 01:33:57.690
While fever is uncomfortable,

01:33:57.690 --> 01:33:59.600
and in fact often involves a mismatch

01:33:59.600 --> 01:34:01.190
between our perception of our shell

01:34:01.190 --> 01:34:03.150
and a perception of our core temperature.

01:34:03.150 --> 01:34:04.090
In other words,

01:34:04.090 --> 01:34:05.920
there are times when our body
temperature is really high,

01:34:05.920 --> 01:34:08.070
we have a fever, and yet we're
shivering and we're cold.

01:34:08.070 --> 01:34:10.370
And that's because under
conditions of fever,

01:34:10.370 --> 01:34:13.720
the immune system
liberates certain molecules

01:34:13.720 --> 01:34:16.540
that impact, and in some ways,

01:34:16.540 --> 01:34:19.900
intentionally disrupt the
preoptic area, the POA,

01:34:19.900 --> 01:34:21.630
and the way it normally functions

01:34:21.630 --> 01:34:24.190
so that it can override peripheral signals

01:34:24.190 --> 01:34:25.770
and simply try and heat the body

01:34:25.770 --> 01:34:29.690
and kill whatever pathogen
has infected the body.

01:34:29.690 --> 01:34:32.450
So for those of you that think about fever

01:34:32.450 --> 01:34:34.540
as always a bad thing, it's not.

01:34:34.540 --> 01:34:36.720
Now, of course we don't want
our core body temperature

01:34:36.720 --> 01:34:39.660
to go so high that tissues of
the brain and body are damage.

01:34:39.660 --> 01:34:43.610
This is one reason why, if
a fever ever goes above 103,

01:34:43.610 --> 01:34:46.490
you need to start getting
a little bit worried, 104.

01:34:46.490 --> 01:34:48.640
There are times when you
need to call an ambulance

01:34:48.640 --> 01:34:49.780
or go to a hospital,

01:34:49.780 --> 01:34:52.070
you really need to employ cooling methods

01:34:52.070 --> 01:34:53.340
of the sort that I talked about before

01:34:53.340 --> 01:34:55.090
to prevent hyperthermia.

01:34:55.090 --> 01:34:57.590
Of course, safe ranges
for body temperature

01:34:57.590 --> 01:34:59.800
vary between infants and adults.

01:34:59.800 --> 01:35:01.150
So you can look those up online,

01:35:01.150 --> 01:35:03.220
depending on the person's
age, what is a safe range,

01:35:03.220 --> 01:35:04.260
what is not.

01:35:04.260 --> 01:35:06.780
But keep in mind that if
you are taking compounds,

01:35:06.780 --> 01:35:08.390
pills to reduce your fever,

01:35:08.390 --> 01:35:10.200
you are actually short circuiting

01:35:10.200 --> 01:35:13.430
the protective mechanism
for burning up the pathogen.

01:35:13.430 --> 01:35:15.460
And that's because most pathogens,

01:35:15.460 --> 01:35:18.860
bacteria and virus, don't survive
well at high temperatures.

01:35:18.860 --> 01:35:19.890
In fact, in laboratories,

01:35:19.890 --> 01:35:21.700
if we want to preserve a virus for use,

01:35:21.700 --> 01:35:23.130
we put it into a freezer.

01:35:23.130 --> 01:35:26.530
If we want to kill a virus,
we heat inoculate it.

01:35:26.530 --> 01:35:28.850
So in many ways, fever
is your natural form

01:35:28.850 --> 01:35:31.230
of heat inoculation
designed to kill pathogens

01:35:31.230 --> 01:35:32.490
of various kinds.

01:35:32.490 --> 01:35:33.980
Now last, but certainly not least,

01:35:33.980 --> 01:35:35.930
I want to refer to the
study that I described

01:35:35.930 --> 01:35:38.320
at the very beginning of this episode,

01:35:38.320 --> 01:35:41.510
involving what's called local hyperthermia

01:35:41.510 --> 01:35:44.340
in order to trigger a number
of biological processes

01:35:44.340 --> 01:35:49.210
in fat tissue in order to
convert white fat to beige fat,

01:35:49.210 --> 01:35:51.786
which is the metabolically
active form of fat.

01:35:51.786 --> 01:35:53.850
Many of you, or at least some of you,

01:35:53.850 --> 01:35:55.060
should be familiar with the fact

01:35:55.060 --> 01:35:57.130
that deliberate cold exposure

01:35:57.130 --> 01:35:59.470
can increase brown fat stores,

01:35:59.470 --> 01:36:02.750
these mitochondrial dense
fat stores that can, in turn,

01:36:02.750 --> 01:36:05.300
allow a person to feel more comfortable

01:36:05.300 --> 01:36:07.830
in cold temperatures, water, or otherwise,

01:36:07.830 --> 01:36:10.370
and increase core metabolism.

01:36:10.370 --> 01:36:12.210
I talked about this in
the episode on cold,

01:36:12.210 --> 01:36:14.530
but very briefly, the
general protocol, again,

01:36:14.530 --> 01:36:17.700
is to get 11 minutes total
per week of uncomfortable,

01:36:17.700 --> 01:36:19.360
yet safe deliberate cold exposure,

01:36:19.360 --> 01:36:21.900
either through ice bath, cold shower,

01:36:21.900 --> 01:36:23.950
cold immersion up to the neck,

01:36:23.950 --> 01:36:26.970
or some other form of cold exposure.

01:36:26.970 --> 01:36:29.220
That triggers increases in brown fat.

01:36:29.220 --> 01:36:32.780
That's been beautifully
shown by Dr. Susanna Søberg.

01:36:32.780 --> 01:36:34.310
And that increase in brown fat

01:36:34.310 --> 01:36:36.320
in turn increases core metabolism

01:36:36.320 --> 01:36:40.100
and one's ability to feel
comfortable in cool temperatures.

01:36:40.100 --> 01:36:42.440
This was a study done in humans,

01:36:42.440 --> 01:36:44.960
and there's now ample
evidence from animal models

01:36:44.960 --> 01:36:47.170
to support that this
is a general phenomenon

01:36:47.170 --> 01:36:50.370
that I think most people
could use and benefit from.

01:36:50.370 --> 01:36:53.470
Local hyperthermia is a
distinctly different phenomenon.

01:36:53.470 --> 01:36:56.700
It involves heating a
particular surface of the body

01:36:56.700 --> 01:37:00.340
as a way to convert the
white fat at that location

01:37:00.340 --> 01:37:04.700
to beige fat, which in turn
leads to more systemic increases

01:37:04.700 --> 01:37:07.990
in thermogenesis and
increases in metabolism,

01:37:07.990 --> 01:37:09.820
and believe it or not, in fat loss.

01:37:09.820 --> 01:37:11.200
Now, the study that I'm referring to

01:37:11.200 --> 01:37:12.850
is a very recent study that was published,

01:37:12.850 --> 01:37:16.050
again, in this terrific
apex journal, Cell,

01:37:16.050 --> 01:37:17.130
Cell Press Journal.

01:37:17.130 --> 01:37:19.110
And again, one of the three top journals,

01:37:19.110 --> 01:37:21.200
Nature, Science, and Cell
are the three top journals.

01:37:21.200 --> 01:37:23.500
Top because they're the most competitive,

01:37:23.500 --> 01:37:25.720
but also generally, not always,

01:37:25.720 --> 01:37:27.100
but generally the most stringent

01:37:27.100 --> 01:37:28.410
in terms of the review process.

01:37:28.410 --> 01:37:30.440
Papers that make it into
these three journals

01:37:30.440 --> 01:37:32.620
generally are of very, very high quality.

01:37:32.620 --> 01:37:34.160
And certainly enough people see them that

01:37:34.160 --> 01:37:35.300
if they're not of high quality,

01:37:35.300 --> 01:37:37.790
they get shot down pretty quickly
in a short amount of time.

01:37:37.790 --> 01:37:39.240
Whereas papers in other journals

01:37:39.240 --> 01:37:40.920
can sometimes last a long time

01:37:40.920 --> 01:37:43.890
before they're ever replicated, et cetera.

01:37:43.890 --> 01:37:44.810
The title of this paper

01:37:44.810 --> 01:37:48.687
is "Local Hyperthermia Therapy
Induces Browning of White Fat

01:37:48.687 --> 01:37:50.850
"and Treats Obesity."

01:37:50.850 --> 01:37:54.440
This was a study that was
performed on mice and humans

01:37:54.440 --> 01:37:55.930
in the same study.

01:37:55.930 --> 01:37:57.820
What this study involved was heating

01:37:57.820 --> 01:38:00.690
of a local patch of skin
to 41 degrees Celsius,

01:38:00.690 --> 01:38:04.045
which is a 105.8 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:38:04.045 --> 01:38:06.750
but not damaging the skin.

01:38:06.750 --> 01:38:08.890
So the methods of heating

01:38:08.890 --> 01:38:10.900
did not involve placing something

01:38:10.900 --> 01:38:12.030
on the skin that would damage it.

01:38:12.030 --> 01:38:13.500
In fact, in the study on the mice,

01:38:13.500 --> 01:38:17.490
they used this kind of
clever molecular chicanery

01:38:17.490 --> 01:38:18.323
in order to do it.

01:38:18.323 --> 01:38:20.260
And in humans, they used a thermocouple

01:38:20.260 --> 01:38:22.930
that would allow them to heat the skin up

01:38:22.930 --> 01:38:25.070
just locally in particular
locations on the body

01:38:25.070 --> 01:38:27.070
that I'll talk about in a moment.

01:38:27.070 --> 01:38:32.070
They refer to this process as
LHT, or local heat therapy.

01:38:33.630 --> 01:38:36.447
The reason they did this
is worth considering.

01:38:36.447 --> 01:38:40.350
It's long been known from clinical data.

01:38:40.350 --> 01:38:42.470
And in fact, from a bit of research data

01:38:42.470 --> 01:38:46.160
that people that
experience burn on a small,

01:38:46.160 --> 01:38:48.900
or unfortunately in some circumstances,

01:38:48.900 --> 01:38:50.980
significant portion of their body

01:38:50.980 --> 01:38:54.303
experience overall decreases in body fat

01:38:54.303 --> 01:38:58.460
and increases in metabolism
that can last many years,

01:38:58.460 --> 01:39:01.810
Now, of course, is not
reasonable nor would one

01:39:01.810 --> 01:39:05.450
ever want to induce burn in
order to induce fat loss.

01:39:05.450 --> 01:39:08.640
But the observed increases
in metabolism and fat loss

01:39:08.640 --> 01:39:11.400
in response to skin surface burn

01:39:11.400 --> 01:39:14.540
couldn't be explained by
reductions in activity

01:39:14.540 --> 01:39:16.253
related to the burn, for instance.

01:39:17.220 --> 01:39:19.280
And in fact, there are molecular pathways

01:39:19.280 --> 01:39:22.160
related to something called UCP1,

01:39:22.160 --> 01:39:24.120
which is uncoupling protein one.

01:39:24.120 --> 01:39:25.860
I talked about this also
in the cold episode,

01:39:25.860 --> 01:39:27.690
but don't worry if you
didn't see that episode,

01:39:27.690 --> 01:39:28.960
or if you choose not to.

01:39:28.960 --> 01:39:33.960
UCP1 has the ability to
increase mitochondrial function

01:39:34.900 --> 01:39:37.240
in ways that increase core
body temperature overall,

01:39:37.240 --> 01:39:39.920
in particular, in beige and brown fat,

01:39:39.920 --> 01:39:42.660
which are these fat cells that exist

01:39:42.660 --> 01:39:44.660
generally along our
spine, and in particular,

01:39:44.660 --> 01:39:46.180
in the upper part of our back

01:39:46.180 --> 01:39:48.020
and around our neck and clavicles.

01:39:48.020 --> 01:39:51.910
And they're responsible for
acting as this sort of a candle,

01:39:51.910 --> 01:39:55.970
or I should say the fuel
or the fat of a candle

01:39:55.970 --> 01:40:00.290
that can be burned up to
manufacture heat in the body.

01:40:00.290 --> 01:40:02.350
So, if you normally think about fat

01:40:02.350 --> 01:40:03.600
and you think about blubbery fat,

01:40:03.600 --> 01:40:05.150
you're thinking about white fat,

01:40:05.150 --> 01:40:06.680
which again is just a storage site.

01:40:06.680 --> 01:40:09.730
Beige fat and brown fat exist
at just a few locations,

01:40:09.730 --> 01:40:13.100
mainly internally, around our
spinal cord and our clavicles,

01:40:13.100 --> 01:40:15.180
and those fat stores are responsible

01:40:15.180 --> 01:40:16.630
for generating heat in our body.

01:40:16.630 --> 01:40:20.246
So they're a very metabolically
active form of fat.

01:40:20.246 --> 01:40:23.840
Small children have a lot
of brown fat and beige fat,

01:40:23.840 --> 01:40:27.320
in particular because very
young children can't shiver.

01:40:27.320 --> 01:40:28.700
A number of you probably didn't know that,

01:40:28.700 --> 01:40:30.390
but very young children can't shiver,

01:40:30.390 --> 01:40:31.890
so they need some way to generate heat

01:40:31.890 --> 01:40:33.520
in order to make sure that they stay alive

01:40:33.520 --> 01:40:35.060
if they were ever to get cold.

01:40:35.060 --> 01:40:37.190
This is also probably the
reason why little kids

01:40:37.190 --> 01:40:39.710
can run around on a cold day
outside without their shirt on

01:40:39.710 --> 01:40:40.880
and they don't even seem to notice,

01:40:40.880 --> 01:40:43.100
whereas adults are freezing cold.

01:40:43.100 --> 01:40:45.780
As we get older, the amount
of beige and brown fat

01:40:45.780 --> 01:40:50.780
tends to either reduce or
shrink or disappear entirely.

01:40:51.120 --> 01:40:53.050
It's still debated which happens.

01:40:53.050 --> 01:40:55.880
But we know that white
fat can be converted

01:40:55.880 --> 01:40:59.557
to this more metabolically
active form of beige fat

01:40:59.557 --> 01:41:02.010
by deliberate cold exposure,

01:41:02.010 --> 01:41:04.060
according to the protocol
I talked about earlier,

01:41:04.060 --> 01:41:07.370
and now it seems, based on this new study,

01:41:07.370 --> 01:41:12.370
that local heating of skin
tissue can also induce UCP1

01:41:12.510 --> 01:41:15.590
and the effects of UCP1 on
increasing mitochondria.

01:41:15.590 --> 01:41:20.380
And in fact, that local
hyperthermia, 41 degrees Celsius,

01:41:20.380 --> 01:41:23.200
that is 105.8 degrees Fahrenheit,

01:41:23.200 --> 01:41:25.890
can actually induce the conversion

01:41:25.890 --> 01:41:28.000
of white fat to beige fat.

01:41:28.000 --> 01:41:29.020
Now that's pretty interesting,

01:41:29.020 --> 01:41:31.290
and I can already predict the
way this is probably going to go

01:41:31.290 --> 01:41:33.380
in the kind of wellness and biohacking

01:41:33.380 --> 01:41:34.410
and longevity communities.

01:41:34.410 --> 01:41:36.610
I'm sure that pretty soon
there are going to be

01:41:36.610 --> 01:41:37.660
people putting heating pads

01:41:37.660 --> 01:41:40.040
on different fat pads
of theirs on their body,

01:41:40.040 --> 01:41:40.940
trying to reduce,

01:41:40.940 --> 01:41:43.600
or at least convert the
white fat into beige fat.

01:41:43.600 --> 01:41:44.740
And who knows, maybe that'll work.

01:41:44.740 --> 01:41:47.310
There have not been many
controlled studies of this yet.

01:41:47.310 --> 01:41:49.730
This is the first, at
least to my knowledge,

01:41:49.730 --> 01:41:53.610
of such studies looking at
this in non-burn conditions.

01:41:53.610 --> 01:41:56.240
Nonetheless, the data are mechanistically

01:41:56.240 --> 01:41:58.720
even more interesting
than this whole business

01:41:58.720 --> 01:42:01.319
about UCP1, and here's why.

01:42:01.319 --> 01:42:03.460
Local hyperthermia,

01:42:03.460 --> 01:42:05.610
using the protocol that
I described before,

01:42:06.630 --> 01:42:09.560
resulted in the increase of a promoter,

01:42:09.560 --> 01:42:11.180
which is essentially a mechanism

01:42:11.180 --> 01:42:14.940
by which certain genes
regulate their activity.

01:42:14.940 --> 01:42:18.980
This is a DNA binding of
something called HSF1.

01:42:18.980 --> 01:42:21.070
We don't have to go too
deep into the mechanism here

01:42:21.070 --> 01:42:21.903
or the nomenclature,

01:42:21.903 --> 01:42:25.100
but HSF stands for heat shock factor one.

01:42:25.100 --> 01:42:30.100
And HSF1 binding to a particular location

01:42:30.811 --> 01:42:35.050
in the genome allowed
for a different molecule

01:42:35.050 --> 01:42:36.170
with a very long name.

01:42:36.170 --> 01:42:37.370
I'll just tell it to you for fun,

01:42:37.370 --> 01:42:40.390
but you can just let the
numbers and letters stream by.

01:42:40.390 --> 01:42:41.796
It's not important.

01:42:41.796 --> 01:42:45.390
HNRNPA2B1, shortened to A2B1,

01:42:45.390 --> 01:42:48.260
which frankly is not that
short to begin with, A2B1,

01:42:48.260 --> 01:42:49.940
is still a name that should be meaningless

01:42:49.940 --> 01:42:52.390
to most everybody, but
here's what's really cool.

01:42:52.390 --> 01:42:57.100
A2B1 is directly involved in
glucose and lipid metabolism

01:42:57.100 --> 01:42:59.519
and regulates the genes that control

01:42:59.519 --> 01:43:01.740
glucose and lipid metabolism.

01:43:01.740 --> 01:43:04.296
So here we have a situation
where local heating of skin

01:43:04.296 --> 01:43:08.210
converted a metabolically
sluggish or inactive cell type,

01:43:08.210 --> 01:43:13.210
the white fat cell, into
the metabolically charging,

01:43:13.600 --> 01:43:16.970
so to speak, beige fat cell, which in turn

01:43:16.970 --> 01:43:20.740
led to systemic, meaning
body-wide increases in metabolism,

01:43:20.740 --> 01:43:21.810
through two mechanism.

01:43:21.810 --> 01:43:24.840
One mechanism is this increase in UCP1,

01:43:24.840 --> 01:43:26.050
which for those of you that want to know,

01:43:26.050 --> 01:43:28.350
UCP1 causes shifts in the way

01:43:28.350 --> 01:43:32.580
that potential energy is
pushed from the protons

01:43:32.580 --> 01:43:33.413
through the mitochondria,

01:43:33.413 --> 01:43:34.940
basically more mitochondrial function,

01:43:34.940 --> 01:43:36.030
which means more ATP,

01:43:36.030 --> 01:43:37.400
which means cells are more active,

01:43:37.400 --> 01:43:39.500
AKA, increased metabolism,

01:43:39.500 --> 01:43:43.350
and increases in things like
heat shock factor one and A2B1,

01:43:43.350 --> 01:43:44.560
which are involved in lipid

01:43:44.560 --> 01:43:46.930
and glucose metabolism and regulation.

01:43:46.930 --> 01:43:48.380
So I want to be very clear,

01:43:48.380 --> 01:43:51.490
this study does not
say that spot reduction

01:43:51.490 --> 01:43:54.070
is possible with local heating of tissue.

01:43:54.070 --> 01:43:56.510
I just can see it now that once this paper

01:43:56.510 --> 01:43:58.740
gets out into the press,
people are going to say,

01:43:58.740 --> 01:44:00.650
oh, heating up a certain patch of skin

01:44:00.650 --> 01:44:02.250
is going to burn fat or convert fat

01:44:02.250 --> 01:44:04.580
to some other cell type at that location.

01:44:04.580 --> 01:44:06.333
Sorry, that's not the way it works.

01:44:07.250 --> 01:44:10.440
They did observe increases
in beige fat cells

01:44:11.330 --> 01:44:13.600
at certain locations in the body.

01:44:13.600 --> 01:44:15.255
But those increases in beige fat

01:44:15.255 --> 01:44:18.750
occurred where beige
fat cells always reside,

01:44:18.750 --> 01:44:20.540
around the spine, the upper neck,

01:44:20.540 --> 01:44:22.430
the clavicles, and so on.

01:44:22.430 --> 01:44:24.290
This is exciting because it provides

01:44:24.290 --> 01:44:26.150
yet another potential mechanism

01:44:26.150 --> 01:44:28.800
in addition to deliberate cold exposure

01:44:28.800 --> 01:44:30.700
to increase beige fat,

01:44:30.700 --> 01:44:34.060
meaning the metabolically
active form of fat cell.

01:44:34.060 --> 01:44:36.570
It also nicely provides a mechanism,

01:44:36.570 --> 01:44:38.390
or at least a potential mechanism,

01:44:38.390 --> 01:44:41.810
for the observation that burn,

01:44:41.810 --> 01:44:44.490
either small patch of skin being burned,

01:44:44.490 --> 01:44:47.856
or again, sadly large
patches of skin being burned,

01:44:47.856 --> 01:44:52.780
leading to these very
extreme and very long lasting

01:44:52.780 --> 01:44:56.600
increases in body fat loss and metabolism.

01:44:56.600 --> 01:44:58.940
What, if anything, should
you do with this information?

01:44:58.940 --> 01:45:02.630
Well, first of all, I want
to very much caution people

01:45:02.630 --> 01:45:05.930
about putting anything
so hot that it can damage

01:45:05.930 --> 01:45:08.220
the surface of your skin onto your skin.

01:45:08.220 --> 01:45:10.060
That would be a terrible idea.

01:45:10.060 --> 01:45:14.370
However, I do predict a
time not too far from now

01:45:14.370 --> 01:45:17.490
where people will start to explore the use

01:45:17.490 --> 01:45:20.020
of local skin heating as a means

01:45:20.020 --> 01:45:24.340
to increase the conversion
of white to beige fat,

01:45:24.340 --> 01:45:26.620
and in turn for beige fat stores

01:45:26.620 --> 01:45:28.200
to increase metabolism overall,

01:45:28.200 --> 01:45:31.690
and maybe even improve glucose
metabolism and thermogenesis.

01:45:31.690 --> 01:45:33.590
If you'd like more detail
else about this study,

01:45:33.590 --> 01:45:36.571
we will provide a link to it
in the show notes caption.

01:45:36.571 --> 01:45:38.520
I should mention that the study,

01:45:38.520 --> 01:45:39.850
at least the portion of the study

01:45:39.850 --> 01:45:41.510
that was focused on humans,

01:45:41.510 --> 01:45:44.733
involved roughly equal
numbers of males and females.

01:45:44.733 --> 01:45:47.456
The subjects followed their
normal daily schedule,

01:45:47.456 --> 01:45:49.810
including time and composition
of meals, they say,

01:45:49.810 --> 01:45:52.010
rest and active hours,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:45:52.900 --> 01:45:54.890
The local hyperthermia therapy was done

01:45:54.890 --> 01:45:55.840
in the following way.

01:45:55.840 --> 01:45:58.170
Here I'm paraphrasing from
their methods section.

01:45:58.170 --> 01:46:00.340
Subjects were seated
in an upright posture.

01:46:00.340 --> 01:46:03.390
They were wearing a standard test robe

01:46:03.390 --> 01:46:05.330
with the head and neck
and shoulders unclothed

01:46:05.330 --> 01:46:08.210
and one meter away from
a thermal imaging camera,

01:46:08.210 --> 01:46:10.380
which could basically
measure the temperature

01:46:10.380 --> 01:46:11.670
at their skin surface to make sure

01:46:11.670 --> 01:46:15.761
that it remained constant
across subjects and yet safe.

01:46:15.761 --> 01:46:18.950
The supraclavicular fat deposits,

01:46:18.950 --> 01:46:21.240
meaning the upper shoulders
and upper back area,

01:46:21.240 --> 01:46:22.660
were exposed to this thermal source,

01:46:22.660 --> 01:46:25.763
again, 41 degrees for 20 minutes.

01:46:26.650 --> 01:46:29.018
So it was 41 degrees for 20 minutes.

01:46:29.018 --> 01:46:31.840
And their core temperatures
and skin temperatures

01:46:31.840 --> 01:46:34.030
were monitored before and after

01:46:34.030 --> 01:46:35.910
this local hyperthermic therapy.

01:46:35.910 --> 01:46:39.130
The subjects were exposed to
this local hyperthermia therapy

01:46:39.130 --> 01:46:41.180
three days per week, separated by day,

01:46:41.180 --> 01:46:42.410
Monday, Wednesday, and Friday,

01:46:42.410 --> 01:46:46.300
so they had weekends off,
for five weeks total,

01:46:46.300 --> 01:46:48.210
after which their data were collected.

01:46:48.210 --> 01:46:49.190
And the study has a number

01:46:49.190 --> 01:46:50.660
of other really interesting features

01:46:50.660 --> 01:46:53.310
that are sure to lead to
increased understanding

01:46:53.310 --> 01:46:55.210
of both mechanism and new protocols,

01:46:55.210 --> 01:46:57.900
such as analysis of the genes and proteins

01:46:57.900 --> 01:46:59.550
that are activated downstream

01:46:59.550 --> 01:47:01.410
of this local hyperthermia therapy.

01:47:01.410 --> 01:47:03.540
I find these data incredibly interesting,

01:47:03.540 --> 01:47:04.960
in part because of the ways

01:47:04.960 --> 01:47:06.610
that local hyperthermia therapy

01:47:06.610 --> 01:47:09.330
mimics deliberate cold exposure therapy.

01:47:09.330 --> 01:47:10.820
Same downstream mechanisms, UCP1,

01:47:10.820 --> 01:47:13.440
and some of the other
pathways are involved,

01:47:13.440 --> 01:47:15.640
and all of that points to a somewhat new,

01:47:15.640 --> 01:47:18.000
but certainly an important concept.

01:47:18.000 --> 01:47:19.800
Many of you have probably
heard of hormesis,

01:47:19.800 --> 01:47:22.530
which is the subjecting of one's self,

01:47:22.530 --> 01:47:23.840
or others, I suppose,

01:47:23.840 --> 01:47:27.300
to enough stress to induce
an adaptation of some kind.

01:47:27.300 --> 01:47:29.800
So hormesis is the reason why

01:47:29.800 --> 01:47:32.056
if you get into cold water repeatedly,

01:47:32.056 --> 01:47:34.810
at first, it's very
painful psychologically,

01:47:34.810 --> 01:47:36.330
and over time you get used to it.

01:47:36.330 --> 01:47:37.470
You never get completely used to it,

01:47:37.470 --> 01:47:38.770
but you get more used to it.

01:47:38.770 --> 01:47:42.020
Hormesis is also used to
describe the adaptation

01:47:42.020 --> 01:47:46.050
to cardiovascular exercise
or to the hard rep sets

01:47:46.050 --> 01:47:48.010
of resistance training
and the growth of muscles

01:47:48.010 --> 01:47:49.200
or the strengthening of muscles

01:47:49.200 --> 01:47:50.780
or the improvement in
cardiovascular function

01:47:50.780 --> 01:47:52.774
to endurance exercise and so forth.

01:47:52.774 --> 01:47:55.170
Hormesis is a somewhat
common term nowadays.

01:47:55.170 --> 01:47:58.000
If you haven't heard
it, now you've heard it.

01:47:58.000 --> 01:48:01.210
In this paper they describe
what is called mitohormesis,

01:48:01.210 --> 01:48:02.750
which is, in essence,

01:48:02.750 --> 01:48:05.720
the fact that any number of
different stressful stimuli,

01:48:05.720 --> 01:48:07.910
provided they activate UCP1

01:48:07.910 --> 01:48:10.100
and some of these other
pathways that I just described,

01:48:10.100 --> 01:48:13.810
like HSF1, can induce
changes in the mitochondria

01:48:13.810 --> 01:48:16.380
that lead to increases in metabolism.

01:48:16.380 --> 01:48:20.300
So it shouldn't surprise
us that cold and heat

01:48:20.300 --> 01:48:23.230
can both lead to increases
in metabolism and conversion

01:48:23.230 --> 01:48:25.300
of white fat to beige fat.

01:48:25.300 --> 01:48:28.840
It shouldn't surprise us because
both pathways are stress.

01:48:28.840 --> 01:48:30.950
Local hyperthermia is stress.

01:48:30.950 --> 01:48:32.840
Burn certainly is stress.

01:48:32.840 --> 01:48:34.988
Sauna is a form of stress.

01:48:34.988 --> 01:48:38.360
Deliberate cold exposure
is a form of stress.

01:48:38.360 --> 01:48:39.790
Exercise is a form of stress.

01:48:39.790 --> 01:48:43.400
And the adaptation to those
stressors is not infinite.

01:48:43.400 --> 01:48:46.570
All of those protocols, any
protocol for that matter,

01:48:46.570 --> 01:48:47.560
is going to be effective

01:48:47.560 --> 01:48:48.710
because it's going to converge

01:48:48.710 --> 01:48:51.730
on an existing internal
biological mechanism.

01:48:51.730 --> 01:48:55.240
So there's no unique
mechanism for each protocol.

01:48:55.240 --> 01:48:57.190
Each protocol that I've
talked about today,

01:48:57.190 --> 01:48:58.920
whether or not it's five
minutes or 20 minutes

01:48:58.920 --> 01:49:00.410
or four times in a day

01:49:00.410 --> 01:49:02.980
or three times per week
or seven times per week

01:49:02.980 --> 01:49:06.500
is tickling or pushing
or stomping, if you will,

01:49:06.500 --> 01:49:08.760
on a given pathway and
really activating it

01:49:08.760 --> 01:49:10.680
to a mild or to a severe degree.

01:49:10.680 --> 01:49:11.660
What I've tried to do

01:49:11.660 --> 01:49:14.060
is to illustrate the general mechanisms

01:49:14.060 --> 01:49:16.210
by which heat in particular

01:49:16.210 --> 01:49:18.460
can activate certain biological pathways

01:49:18.460 --> 01:49:20.410
so that you can devise protocols

01:49:20.410 --> 01:49:23.140
that are going to be optimal
for you and your needs.

01:49:23.140 --> 01:49:24.973
So just to briefly recap,

01:49:25.980 --> 01:49:28.620
if you want to get the greatest
growth hormone increases,

01:49:28.620 --> 01:49:33.170
do sauna or other deliberate
heat exposure fairly seldom,

01:49:33.170 --> 01:49:36.050
probably no more than once
per week, maybe even less,

01:49:36.050 --> 01:49:37.650
and do it a lot that day.

01:49:37.650 --> 01:49:39.820
Just make sure that you break
it up into multiple sessions.

01:49:39.820 --> 01:49:42.450
In the study I described
earlier they did four sessions,

01:49:42.450 --> 01:49:43.600
30 minutes each.

01:49:43.600 --> 01:49:46.670
But that was just once a week.

01:49:46.670 --> 01:49:49.130
If you're interested in
the cardiovascular benefits

01:49:49.130 --> 01:49:51.640
and the potential longevity
benefits of sauna,

01:49:51.640 --> 01:49:53.930
well then it's clear that
doing it three to four,

01:49:53.930 --> 01:49:55.180
maybe even seven times per week

01:49:55.180 --> 01:49:56.520
is going to be more beneficial

01:49:56.520 --> 01:49:58.860
than doing it just one
or three times per week.

01:49:58.860 --> 01:50:00.660
It stands to reason that
for those of you interested

01:50:00.660 --> 01:50:03.389
in the general health effects of sauna,

01:50:03.389 --> 01:50:06.510
about an hour per week
broken up into three sessions

01:50:06.510 --> 01:50:09.350
makes the most sense based
on my read of the data.

01:50:09.350 --> 01:50:12.060
And again, that range of
80 to 100 degrees Celsius

01:50:12.060 --> 01:50:13.650
is going to be your guide.

01:50:13.650 --> 01:50:15.790
And in terms of the
mental health benefits,

01:50:15.790 --> 01:50:18.270
it seems that getting a
little bit uncomfortable

01:50:18.270 --> 01:50:21.020
in that heat environment,
sauna or otherwise,

01:50:21.020 --> 01:50:23.580
provided it's safe, is
going to be the best way

01:50:23.580 --> 01:50:25.510
to access those mental health effects

01:50:25.510 --> 01:50:29.400
by way of increasing dynorphine,
which, as you recall,

01:50:29.400 --> 01:50:32.270
will then increase the
ability of endorphin

01:50:32.270 --> 01:50:34.300
to have its positive effects on mood

01:50:34.300 --> 01:50:36.280
after you get out of the sauna

01:50:36.280 --> 01:50:37.970
or other deliberate heat exposure.

01:50:37.970 --> 01:50:41.441
And in terms of timing,
after a workout of any kind,

01:50:41.441 --> 01:50:43.300
morning or afternoon,

01:50:43.300 --> 01:50:46.170
or if you're not doing it after a workout,

01:50:46.170 --> 01:50:47.640
certainly in the later part of the day

01:50:47.640 --> 01:50:48.820
is going to be most beneficial

01:50:48.820 --> 01:50:50.250
as it relates to sleep.

01:50:50.250 --> 01:50:51.840
But of course, there's a caveat there,

01:50:51.840 --> 01:50:53.240
which I will mention again,

01:50:53.240 --> 01:50:54.480
which is that for those of you

01:50:54.480 --> 01:50:56.310
that have no trouble sleeping
'cause you're exhausted

01:50:56.310 --> 01:50:58.420
or you're just one of
these phenomenal sleepers,

01:50:58.420 --> 01:51:00.498
well then do it any time of day or night.

01:51:00.498 --> 01:51:02.910
But for most people
doing it later in the day

01:51:02.910 --> 01:51:04.040
is going to be more beneficial

01:51:04.040 --> 01:51:07.000
because of the post sauna cooling effect

01:51:07.000 --> 01:51:10.710
and the relationship between
cooling by a degree or more

01:51:10.710 --> 01:51:12.140
as a way to enter sleep.

01:51:12.140 --> 01:51:14.160
Thank you for joining me
today for my discussion

01:51:14.160 --> 01:51:18.330
about the science of heat
and heating for health.

